/* reglib1: FEDERAL mortgage/real-estate regulation reference library for the VANESSA dashboard. Plain data, no functions. Written 2026-10-31 for Steven Shearrill (Patriot Pacific Financial Corp, NMLS 1921615; CA/IL/NV/FL/AZ). Entries marked verify:true contain items that could not be confirmed against a primary source on the write date. Dollar thresholds that index annually are stated for the year named in the text. */
window.REG_LIB=(window.REG_LIB||[]).concat([
{k:"tila_regz",n:"Truth in Lending Act / Regulation Z",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1601 et seq.; 12 CFR Part 1026",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination","advertising","closing"],summary:"TILA requires lenders and brokers to disclose the true cost of credit (APR, finance charge, amount financed, total of payments) so consumers can shop. Regulation Z implements it and also houses the mortgage-specific rules: TRID disclosures, ability-to-repay, loan originator compensation, HOEPA, higher-priced mortgage loan escrows and appraisals, and advertising rules. It applies to consumer-purpose credit secured by a dwelling regardless of who holds the license. Most mortgage enforcement actions against brokers cite some part of Reg Z.",applies:"Any creditor or mortgage broker that arranges consumer-purpose, dwelling-secured credit; business-purpose loans (true investor DSCR loans on non-owner-occupied property) are exempt under 1026.3(a) but the exemption must be documented.",key:["APR must be disclosed within 1/8 of 1 percent accuracy for regular transactions (1026.22); finance charge tolerance is $100 on the Closing Disclosure (1026.38 / 1026.19(f)).","Right of rescission: 3 business days after consummation on refinances and HELOCs secured by the principal dwelling, extended to 3 years if notices or material disclosures are defective (1026.23).","Advertising trigger terms: stating a rate, payment amount, down payment or number of payments requires the full set of terms including APR (1026.24); only the APR may be stated as a simple annual rate alongside it.","Higher-priced mortgage loan (HPML) = APR 1.5 points over APOR for first liens (2.5 for jumbo, 3.5 for junior liens); requires a 5-year escrow account and, for most, a full interior appraisal (1026.35).","Prohibited acts on dwelling-secured loans: coercing appraisers, steering, dual compensation, financing single-premium credit insurance, mandatory arbitration (1026.36).","ARM disclosures: initial rate adjustment notice 210-240 days before the first payment change and 60-120 days before later changes (1026.20(c)-(d)).","Periodic statements required for closed-end mortgages (1026.41) with servicer exemptions for small servicers (5,000 or fewer loans).","Payoff statements must be sent within 7 business days of request (1026.36(c)(3)); payments credited as of receipt date.","Escrow account exemption for small creditors in rural/underserved areas with assets under the annual threshold (about $2.7 billion for 2026, verify).","Record retention: 2 years for most evidence of compliance, 3 years for LO compensation and ATR records (1026.25)."],broker:"Steven must make sure every consumer-purpose file carries accurate TRID disclosures (he usually relies on the wholesale lender to issue them, but the broker is responsible for the LE if he issues it). He must run the HPML test on every quote so escrow and appraisal requirements are known before locking. Every ad, social post, text or flyer that mentions a rate or payment needs the Reg Z trigger-term package. On refinances he should calendar the 3-day rescission window and never let funds disburse early. Investor DSCR files need a signed business-purpose certification and property-use evidence to stay outside Reg Z.",penalties:"Actual damages plus statutory damages of twice the finance charge ($400 minimum, $4,000 maximum per loan; higher in class actions), attorney fees, extended rescission up to 3 years; CFPB civil penalties up to about $7,000 per day (tier 1) to about $1.4 million per day (tier 3, knowing); state AGs may also sue.",eff:"Enacted 1968; Reg Z moved to CFPB 2011; major mortgage amendments 2013-2014; thresholds adjust each January.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1026 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026"},{t:"CFPB TILA page",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1026/"}],verify:false},
{k:"respa_regx",n:"Real Estate Settlement Procedures Act / Regulation X",cite:"12 U.S.C. 2601 et seq.; 12 CFR Part 1024",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination","closing","servicing"],summary:"RESPA governs the settlement process on federally related mortgage loans: what must be disclosed, who may be paid, and how escrow accounts and servicing transfers are handled. Its best-known rule is Section 8, which bans kickbacks and unearned fees for referrals of settlement business. Regulation X also contains the mortgage servicing rules (error resolution, loss mitigation, force-placed insurance). Nearly every residential purchase or refinance is a federally related mortgage loan.",applies:"Brokers, lenders, title and escrow companies, real estate agents and anyone who gives or receives a thing of value for referring settlement business on a 1-4 family residential mortgage; excludes true business-purpose loans and loans on 25+ acres.",key:["Section 8(a): no fee, kickback or thing of value for referring settlement service business (12 CFR 1024.14(b)).","Section 8(b): no split of a charge except for services actually performed (1024.14(c)); marking up third-party fees is unlawful.","Affiliated business arrangement disclosure required at or before referral, on the prescribed form, with no required use (1024.15; 12 U.S.C. 2607(c)(4)).","Marketing services agreements and lead purchases must be paid at fair market value for actual services, not referrals (CFPB Advisory Opinion Feb 2023 on digital comparison platforms).","Special Information Booklet (Your Home Loan Toolkit) within 3 business days of application on purchases (1024.6).","List of homeownership counseling organizations within 3 business days of application (1024.20).","Escrow: initial escrow statement within 45 days of settlement; annual analysis; cushion limited to 1/6 of annual disbursements (1024.17).","Servicing transfer notice 15 days before transfer by transferor and within 15 days after by transferee; 60-day grace period for misdirected payments (1024.33).","Section 9: seller may not require a particular title company as a condition of sale (12 U.S.C. 2608).","Section 10 escrow limits; Section 6 qualified written request response: acknowledge in 5 business days, resolve in 30 (1024.35-1024.36).","Record retention 5 years for most RESPA documents (1024.10(e))."],broker:"Steven cannot pay or receive anything of value for referrals: no gift cards to agents for sends, no paid 'desk rental' that is really a referral fee, no co-marketing where he pays more than his share of the actual cost. If his real estate brokerage and mortgage company both touch a transaction he must give the AfBA disclosure at the time of referral and may not require use of either. Any lead-generation or MSA contract needs a fair-market-value analysis in writing. He should keep RESPA files for 5 years and document every third-party fee at cost.",penalties:"Section 8 violations: criminal fine up to $10,000 and 1 year imprisonment; civil liability to the consumer of 3 times the charge paid plus attorney fees; CFPB and state civil penalties; license discipline. Servicing violations: actual damages, statutory damages up to $2,000 per pattern-or-practice violation.",eff:"Enacted 1974; Regulation X to CFPB 2011; servicing rules effective Jan 10, 2014 with amendments 2016-2021.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1024 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1024"},{t:"CFPB RESPA page",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1024/"}],verify:false},
{k:"trid",n:"TILA-RESPA Integrated Disclosure Rule (TRID / Know Before You Owe)",cite:"12 CFR 1026.19(e)-(f), 1026.37, 1026.38",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination","closing"],summary:"TRID replaced the GFE, early TIL, HUD-1 and final TIL with the Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure for most closed-end consumer mortgages. It fixes when the forms must go out, what counts as an application, which fees can change and by how much, and when the consumer must receive the final numbers before closing. It is the rule most likely to delay a closing if mishandled. In July 2026 the CFPB opened a request for information on simplifying TRID timing, but the current rule remains in force.",applies:"Creditors and mortgage brokers on closed-end consumer credit secured by real property or a cooperative unit; HELOCs, reverse mortgages and mobile-home-only loans are excluded.",key:["Application = six items: name, income, SSN, property address, estimated value, loan amount (1026.2(a)(3)); once received, the LE clock starts.","Loan Estimate delivered or mailed within 3 business days of application and at least 7 business days before consummation (1026.19(e)(1)(iii)).","No fee other than a reasonable credit report fee may be charged before the consumer receives the LE and indicates intent to proceed (1026.19(e)(2)).","Zero tolerance: lender/broker fees, transfer taxes, fees for services the consumer cannot shop for; 10 percent aggregate tolerance: recording fees and shoppable services chosen from the written provider list; unlimited: prepaid interest, insurance, impounds, services from a provider not on the list (1026.19(e)(3)).","Revised LE only for a valid changed circumstance, delivered within 3 business days of learning of the change and no later than 4 business days before consummation (1026.19(e)(4)).","Closing Disclosure must be received at least 3 business days before consummation; mailed CDs are deemed received 3 business days after mailing (1026.19(f)(1)).","A new 3-business-day wait is triggered if APR moves beyond tolerance (1/8 or 1/4 percent), the loan product changes, or a prepayment penalty is added (1026.19(f)(2)(ii)).","Tolerance cures: refund within 60 days of consummation with a corrected CD (1026.19(f)(2)(v)).","Written list of service providers must accompany the LE when the consumer may shop (1026.19(e)(1)(vi)).","Business day for the LE means days the creditor is open; for the CD waiting period it means all days except Sundays and federal holidays.","Record retention: CD and related records 5 years; LE 3 years (1026.25(c))."],broker:"Steven should log the date and time he receives the sixth application item and make sure the lender's LE goes out within 3 business days (he must not collect an appraisal fee or application fee before intent to proceed). He needs a written changed-circumstance note in the file every time a fee or loan term moves. He should back-plan every closing from the CD receipt date, remembering mailed or e-signed-but-unacknowledged CDs add 3 days. He should avoid last-minute program switches that trigger a new 3-day wait, and keep his service provider list current for his CA, NV, AZ, FL and IL closings.",penalties:"TILA statutory damages and rescission exposure for disclosure errors; CFPB civil penalties; secondary-market repurchase demands and unsalable loans; state license discipline.",eff:"Effective Oct 3, 2015; amended Oct 2017 (TRID 2.0) and April 2018 (black hole fix); CFPB RFI on simplification issued July 2026.",src:[{t:"CFPB TRID resources",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/tila-respa-integrated-disclosures/"},{t:"CFPB RFI on TRID, rescission and reverse mortgages (July 2026)",u:"https://www.consumerfinancemonitor.com/2026/07/13/cfpb-issues-rfi-on-trid-rule-rescission-and-reverse-mortgages/"}],verify:false},
{k:"ecoa_regb",n:"Equal Credit Opportunity Act / Regulation B",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1691 et seq.; 12 CFR Part 1002",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["fair-lending","origination","appraisal"],summary:"ECOA prohibits discrimination in any aspect of a credit transaction on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex (including sexual orientation and gender identity), marital status, age, receipt of public assistance income, or good-faith exercise of consumer-protection rights. Regulation B adds procedural rules: how applications are taken, what may be asked, when adverse action notices and appraisal copies must be sent, and what records must be kept. It covers business and consumer credit and applies to brokers as well as lenders.",applies:"Every creditor, including mortgage brokers who regularly participate in credit decisions or refer applicants; applies to pre-qualification conversations, not just formal applications.",key:["Prohibited bases: race, color, religion, national origin, sex, marital status, age, public assistance income, exercise of rights under the Consumer Credit Protection Act (1002.2(z), 1002.4).","Discouragement is a violation even if no application is taken (1002.4(b)); statements like 'that area is hard to lend in' are classic discouragement.","May not ask about spouse unless spouse will be liable or the applicant relies on spousal income or lives in a community property state (1002.5(c)); CA, NV, AZ are community property states.","Must consider alimony, child support, part-time income and public assistance if the applicant chooses to disclose it (1002.6(b)(5)).","Adverse action notice within 30 days of a completed application, stating specific principal reasons (1002.9); counteroffers not accepted within 90 days also need a notice.","Incomplete application: notice of incompleteness or adverse action within 30 days (1002.9(c)).","Appraisal copies: provide copies of all valuations promptly on completion or 3 business days before consummation, whichever is earlier; notice of right to copies within 3 business days of application (1002.14).","Government monitoring information (HMDA demographic data) must be requested on dwelling-secured applications (1002.13).","Record retention 25 months for consumer credit (1002.12).","Spousal signature: cannot require a non-applicant spouse to sign the note; may require signature on the deed of trust to perfect the lien (1002.7(d)).","Special purpose credit programs permitted under 1002.8 (used by several lenders for first-time buyer programs)."],broker:"Steven must give the same information, programs and pricing to every inquiry and keep notes showing it. He should send or make sure the lender sends an adverse action notice when a borrower is declined or walks away after a counteroffer. He must hand over appraisal and AVM copies promptly and track the 3-day pre-closing deadline. In his community-property states he may ask about a spouse only within the Reg B limits. His CRM should be reviewed for discouragement language, and he should document the reason any lead is not pursued.",penalties:"Actual damages, punitive damages up to $10,000 per individual action (or the lesser of $500,000 or 1 percent of net worth in class actions), attorney fees; CFPB, DOJ and HUD referrals; pattern-or-practice suits by DOJ.",eff:"Enacted 1974; Reg B to CFPB 2011; valuations rule effective Jan 18, 2014; CFPB 2021 interpretive rule on sexual orientation and gender identity.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1002 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1002"},{t:"CFPB ECOA page",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1002/"}],verify:false},
{k:"fcra",n:"Fair Credit Reporting Act / Regulation V",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1681 et seq.; 12 CFR Part 1022",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["privacy","origination","data-security"],summary:"FCRA governs who may pull a consumer report, for what purpose, and what must happen after. A mortgage broker is a user of consumer reports and must have a permissible purpose and written or clear authorization before ordering credit. It also sets the rules for risk-based pricing notices, credit score disclosures, disputes, disposal of report information, and prescreened offers (the source of trigger leads). The 2025 Homebuyers Privacy Protection Act amended FCRA to sharply limit mortgage trigger leads starting March 2026.",applies:"Any person who obtains or uses a consumer report, furnishes information to a credit bureau, or receives prescreened lists; brokers are users and sometimes resellers.",key:["Permissible purpose required before any pull (1681b); for a mortgage, the consumer's authorization and a credit transaction initiated by the consumer.","Notice to home loan applicant with credit score disclosure (1681g(g)) on every 1-4 family dwelling-secured application; usually bundled with the credit report.","Risk-based pricing notice or the credit score exception notice (12 CFR 1022.70-.75).","Adverse action based in whole or part on a consumer report requires FCRA notice content: bureau name, right to free report within 60 days, right to dispute (1681m).","Disposal rule: shred or securely destroy credit report information (12 CFR 1022.82 / 16 CFR 682).","Furnisher duties: accuracy and investigation of disputes within 30 days (1681s-2).","Prescreened offers must carry an opt-out notice and the consumer may opt out via 888-5-OPTOUT (1681b(c), 1681m(d)).","Reseller of reports (e.g., re-issuing a tri-merge to a lender) carries reseller duties under 1681e(e).","Medical information may not be used in credit decisions except as allowed by 1022.30.","Identity theft red flags and address discrepancy rules (1022.82, 1022.90) attach to brokers that maintain covered accounts.","Trigger leads: from March 5, 2026 bureaus may furnish a mortgage-inquiry prescreened list only if the recipient has a firm offer and either the consumer authorized it or the recipient has an existing account/servicing/origination relationship (Homebuyers Privacy Protection Act, Pub. L. 119-37, verify citation)."],broker:"Steven must get a signed or clearly recorded credit authorization (including for soft pulls) before ordering any report and keep it in the file. He should confirm the credit vendor delivers the score disclosure and the home loan applicant notice, and he should issue FCRA-compliant adverse action content when a decline is based on credit. He should shred or securely delete old reports and never forward a report to an unlicensed third party. He must not buy trigger leads from vendors who cannot show HPPA compliance, and he can tell borrowers to opt out at OptOutPrescreen.",penalties:"Negligent violations: actual damages plus attorney fees; willful violations: statutory damages $100-$1,000 per consumer plus punitive damages; FTC/CFPB civil penalties up to about $5,000 per violation (indexed); criminal penalties for obtaining a report under false pretenses (2 years).",eff:"Enacted 1970; FACTA amendments 2003; Reg V to CFPB 2011; Homebuyers Privacy Protection Act signed Sept 5, 2025, effective March 5, 2026.",src:[{t:"15 U.S.C. 1681 (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/chapter-41/subchapter-III"},{t:"NAMB: Understanding the Trigger Leads Ban",u:"https://www.namb.org/understanding-the-trigger-leads-ban"}],verify:false},
{k:"hmda_regc",n:"Home Mortgage Disclosure Act / Regulation C",cite:"12 U.S.C. 2801 et seq.; 12 CFR Part 1003",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["fair-lending","origination"],summary:"HMDA requires covered lenders to collect and report loan-level data on applications, originations and purchases of dwelling-secured loans so regulators and the public can spot discriminatory or redlining patterns. The data includes applicant demographics, pricing, credit characteristics and the action taken. Mortgage brokers who do not make credit decisions are generally not reporters, but they must collect the demographic information that the funding lender reports. Thresholds for who must report are adjusted yearly.",applies:"Banks, credit unions and nonbank lenders that originated at least 25 closed-end mortgages (or 200 open-end lines) in each of the two preceding years and meet the asset and location tests; brokers are covered only if they fund in their own name and make the credit decision.",key:["Closed-end reporting threshold: 25 originations in each of the prior two calendar years (court vacated the 100-loan threshold in Sept 2022; CFPB confirmed 25 in Dec 2022).","Open-end threshold: 200 lines in each of the prior two years (permanent since Jan 1, 2022).","Asset-size exemption for depository institutions: $59 million for 2026 data (adjusted each year).","Data points: 48 fields under the 2015 rule including age, credit score, DTI, CLTV, rate spread, AUS result, NMLS ID of the loan originator (1003.4).","Demographic information collected on the URLA addendum; applicant may decline, and the lender must then record by visual observation or surname for in-person applications (1003.4(a)(10), Appendix B).","LAR submitted to the CFPB by March 1 following the calendar year; quarterly reporting for lenders with 60,000+ records.","Modified LAR and disclosure statement must be available to the public on request (1003.5).","Partial exemptions for small-volume insured depositories under EGRRCPA (fewer than 500 closed-end loans).","Record retention 3 years (1003.5(a)).","Business-purpose loans on dwellings are reportable unless for a non-dwelling purpose; most DSCR investor loans are reportable by the lender."],broker:"Steven does not file a LAR as a broker, but each application he takes must include the demographic section properly completed so his wholesale lenders can report. He should record the NMLS ID of the originator on every file and never leave the demographic fields blank without the borrower's declination. If Patriot Pacific ever funds loans in its own name (mini-correspondent or warehouse) he must count originations each year against the 25-loan test and build HMDA reporting into the process. He should also know HMDA data is public, so his lenders' fair-lending exposure is visible to regulators and plaintiffs.",penalties:"Civil money penalties by the CFPB or prudential regulator; resubmission orders; fair-lending referrals to DOJ; reputational harm from public data.",eff:"Enacted 1975; Reg C to CFPB 2011; 2015 rule effective Jan 1, 2018; closed-end threshold reverted to 25 in 2022; 2026 asset exemption $59 million (eff. Jan 7, 2026).",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1003 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1003"},{t:"CFPB 2026 HMDA asset exemption",u:"https://www.consumerfinancemonitor.com/2026/01/07/cfpb-adjusts-hmda-asset-exemption-threshold-2/"}],verify:false},
{k:"safe_act",n:"Secure and Fair Enforcement for Mortgage Licensing Act (SAFE Act) / Regulations G and H",cite:"12 U.S.C. 5101 et seq.; 12 CFR Parts 1007-1008",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB (standards) / NMLS and state regulators (licensing)",topic:["licensing","origination"],summary:"The SAFE Act created the national licensing and registration framework for mortgage loan originators through the Nationwide Multistate Licensing System (NMLS). Every individual who takes a residential mortgage application or negotiates terms for compensation must be either state-licensed (nonbank) or federally registered (bank employee) and hold a unique NMLS identifier. States must meet minimum standards for education, testing, background checks and bonding. The Act also created temporary authority to operate while a new state license is pending.",applies:"Individuals who take residential mortgage loan applications or offer or negotiate terms for compensation or gain, and the companies that sponsor them; real estate brokers who only refer are not MLOs unless they negotiate loan terms.",key:["State-licensed MLO must complete 20 hours of NMLS-approved pre-licensing education including 3 hours federal law, 3 hours ethics, 2 hours nontraditional products, plus state electives (12 CFR 1008.105).","Pass the SAFE MLO National Test with Uniform State Content (UST); score 75 percent; retake waits of 30, 30 then 180 days (1008.107).","8 hours of continuing education each year: 3 federal law, 2 ethics, 2 nontraditional, 1 elective, plus state-specific hours (1008.107(e)); no credit for repeating the same course in successive years.","Background: fingerprints, FBI check, credit report authorization; no felony in prior 7 years and never for fraud, dishonesty, breach of trust or money laundering (1008.105(b)).","Financial responsibility and surety bond or net worth per state (1008.105(c)-(d)).","Unique NMLS identifier must appear on all residential loan documents and marketing (12 CFR 1026.36(g)).","Annual renewal Nov 1 - Dec 31; licenses lapse if CE is not posted and attested.","Temporary authority to operate: up to 120 days when moving between states or from a bank registration, if licensed in good standing in another state for the prior 30 days or registered for 1 year (12 U.S.C. 5117).","Company (sponsor) must be licensed in each state and sponsor each MLO in NMLS; branch licensing where required.","Employment and disclosure history must be kept current in the MU4 within 30 days of changes."],broker:"Steven holds licenses in CA, IL, NV, FL and AZ, so each year he must complete 8 hours of national CE plus any state hours (CA elective 1 hour, FL 2 hours FL law; verify NV, AZ, IL specifics) and renew all five by Dec 31. He must put NMLS 1921615 (company) and his own NMLS ID on every ad, business card, email signature, LE and 1003. Any new MLO he brings into Patriot Pacific must be sponsored in NMLS before taking an application. He should keep his MU4 current (address, other business, disclosure questions) and watch the CA DRE endorsement and DFPI distinctions separately.",penalties:"Unlicensed origination is a state crime in most states and voids compensation in some; CFPB and state civil penalties; license revocation; loans brokered by unlicensed MLOs are often unsaleable.",eff:"Enacted July 30, 2008; Regs G and H to CFPB 2011; temporary authority effective Nov 24, 2019.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1008 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1008"},{t:"NMLS Resource Center",u:"https://mortgage.nationwidelicensingsystem.org/"}],verify:false},
{k:"dodd_frank",n:"Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (Title X and Title XIV)",cite:"Pub. L. 111-203; 12 U.S.C. 5301 et seq., 5481 et seq.; 15 U.S.C. 1639b-1639h",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination","servicing","fair-lending","appraisal"],summary:"Dodd-Frank created the CFPB (Title X) and rewrote mortgage law in Title XIV, the Mortgage Reform and Anti-Predatory Lending Act. Title XIV is the statutory source of ability-to-repay, qualified mortgage, loan originator compensation and anti-steering, appraisal independence, HPML appraisals, expanded HOEPA, servicing standards and the UDAAP prohibition. The CFPB implements it mostly through Regulations Z, X and B. The 2025-2026 period has seen the CFPB's funding cut to 6.5 percent of Fed operating expenses and large staff reductions, but the statutes and rules remain in force and are enforceable by states.",applies:"All creditors, mortgage brokers and loan originators on residential mortgage loans; the CFPB supervises nonbank mortgage companies of any size.",key:["Section 1411-1412: ability-to-repay and qualified mortgage (15 U.S.C. 1639c).","Section 1403: loan originator compensation may not vary with loan terms; no dual compensation; anti-steering (1639b(c)).","Section 1402: originators must be qualified, licensed and include NMLS ID on documents.","Section 1414: limits on prepayment penalties (none on non-QM or ARMs; max 3 years, 3-2-1 percent on QMs).","Section 1431-1433: HOEPA expanded to purchase loans and HELOCs with lower triggers.","Section 1471-1474: appraisal independence, HPML appraisals, AVM standards, appraisal copies.","Section 1024(a)(1)(A): CFPB supervisory authority over all nonbank mortgage originators, brokers and servicers.","Section 1031/1036: UDAAP (unfair, deceptive or abusive acts or practices) enforced by CFPB; Section 1042 lets state AGs enforce federal consumer financial law.","Section 1022: CFPB rulemaking; Section 1013(b)(3): consumer complaint database.","Section 1461-1465: mandatory escrows on HPMLs; 1463: servicing standards (QWR, force-placed insurance)."],broker:"For Steven, Dodd-Frank is the reason his comp plan cannot vary by rate or product, why he must document ability-to-repay, and why the CFPB and California DFPI can examine a two-person brokerage. He should keep a written compensation plan, anti-steering safe-harbor documentation (three-loan-option presentation) on any lender-paid file, and treat complaints seriously because the CFPB complaint portal is public. He should assume state regulators and AGs will fill enforcement gaps while the CFPB is downsized.",penalties:"CFPB civil penalties in three tiers (about $7,000, $35,000 and $1.4 million per day, indexed); restitution; consent orders; private TILA remedies for Title XIV violations; state AG enforcement under Section 1042.",eff:"Signed July 21, 2010; CFPB transfer date July 21, 2011; Title XIV rules effective Jan 10, 2014; CFPB funding cap reduced to 6.5 percent by the 2025 reconciliation act.",src:[{t:"Dodd-Frank Act text (govinfo)",u:"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-111publ203/pdf/PLAW-111publ203.pdf"},{t:"Covington: The CFPB in 2026",u:"https://www.cov.com/-/media/files/corporate/publications/2026/01/funding-haze-and-deregulatory-pursuits-the-cfpb-in-2026.pdf"}],verify:false},
{k:"atr_qm",n:"Ability-to-Repay / Qualified Mortgage Rule",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1639c; 12 CFR 1026.43",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination"],summary:"A creditor may not make a residential mortgage loan unless it makes a reasonable, good-faith determination that the consumer can repay it, based on verified income, assets, debts and the fully indexed payment. A qualified mortgage (QM) gets a presumption of compliance: a safe harbor if the APR is below APOR plus 1.5 points (first lien) or a rebuttable presumption if higher. Since 2021 the General QM is defined by price rather than a 43 percent DTI, and the GSE patch expired in 2021-2022. The CFPB's 2026 agenda lists ATR/QM as a pre-rule item, so changes may be proposed but none are in effect.",applies:"Creditors on closed-end consumer credit secured by a dwelling; brokers must deliver complete verified documentation but the ATR determination is the creditor's. Exempt: HELOCs, reverse mortgages, bridge loans of 12 months or less, construction phase, business-purpose loans.",key:["Eight ATR factors: income/assets, employment, monthly payment on the loan, simultaneous loans, mortgage-related obligations, other debts, DTI or residual income, credit history (1026.43(c)(2)).","Verification with third-party records: W-2s, tax returns, pay stubs, bank statements, VOE (1026.43(c)(3)-(4)); Appendix Q was eliminated in 2021.","ARM payment must be calculated at the fully indexed rate or introductory rate, whichever is higher, with substantially equal payments (1026.43(c)(5)).","General QM: APR not more than 2.25 points over APOR (higher spreads for small loans), points and fees within the cap, no neg-am, interest-only or balloon, term 30 years or less, consider and verify income and debts (1026.43(e)(2)).","Points-and-fees cap: 3 percent of loan amount for loans of about $130,461 or more in 2025 (2026 figure adjusts, verify), with higher percentage caps for smaller loans.","Safe harbor if APR is less than APOR + 1.5 (first lien) / 3.5 (subordinate); rebuttable presumption above that (1026.43(e)(1)).","Seasoned QM: fixed-rate first lien held in portfolio 36 months with no more than two 30-day lates and no 60-day lates (1026.43(e)(7)).","Small creditor portfolio QM and balloon-payment QM for creditors under $2.7 billion in assets and 2,000 loans (1026.43(e)(5)-(6)).","Prepayment penalties allowed only on fixed-rate QMs, max 3 years, 2-2-1 percent, with an alternative no-penalty offer (1026.43(g)).","Record retention 3 years after consummation (1026.25(c)(3)); liability for ATR violations runs 3 years as an action and indefinitely as a defense to foreclosure by recoupment."],broker:"Steven must gather and verify the full income and liability picture before submitting, including qualifying ARM borrowers at the fully indexed rate. He should know the points-and-fees cap on every file because broker compensation counts toward it on lender-paid transactions and total compensation on borrower-paid. For non-QM and DSCR loans he should document the business-purpose exemption or the lender's ATR analysis. He should watch for CFPB proposals in late 2026 but keep underwriting to the current rule.",penalties:"ATR violation: TILA statutory damages plus all finance charges and fees paid in the first 3 years, plus actual damages and attorney fees; recoupment defense in foreclosure for the life of the loan; CFPB penalties.",eff:"Effective Jan 10, 2014; General QM price-based definition mandatory for applications on or after Oct 1, 2022 (optional from March 1, 2021); seasoned QM effective March 1, 2021.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1026.43 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026/subpart-E/section-1026.43"},{t:"CFPB ATR/QM resources",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/ability-to-repay-and-qualified-mortgage-rule/"}],verify:false},
{k:"lo_comp",n:"Loan Originator Compensation and Anti-Steering Rule",cite:"12 CFR 1026.36(d)-(f), (g), (h), (i)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination","licensing"],summary:"The LO comp rule says a loan originator (individual or brokerage) may not be paid based on the terms of a loan other than the amount of credit extended, and may not be paid by both the consumer and another party on the same transaction. It bans steering a consumer to a loan that pays the originator more unless it is in the consumer's interest, and provides a safe harbor when the originator presents loan options from a meaningful number of creditors. It also requires qualification, training and NMLS ID disclosure, bans mandatory arbitration, and bars financing single-premium credit insurance.",applies:"Mortgage brokers, brokerage companies, individual loan originators and creditors on closed-end consumer credit secured by a dwelling (HELOCs excluded from the comp provisions).",key:["No compensation based on a term of a transaction (rate, fees, prepayment penalty, product type) or a proxy for a term (1026.36(d)(1)); loan amount is allowed.","Permissible factors: overall volume, long-term performance, quality of files, pull-through, hourly pay, new vs. existing customers (Comment 36(d)(1)-3).","Dual compensation ban: if the consumer pays the broker, no creditor or other party may pay the broker on that loan, and the brokerage may still pay its individual LO (1026.36(d)(2)).","Pricing concessions by an individual LO are generally prohibited; exception to cover unforeseen settlement cost increases (Comment 36(d)(1)-7).","Compensation plan changes must be prospective; periodic changes to a lender-paid comp level are allowed but not loan-by-loan.","Anti-steering: no steering to a loan that produces greater compensation unless in the consumer's interest (1026.36(e)(1)); safe harbor by presenting lowest rate, lowest rate without risky features, and lowest total origination charges from at least 3 creditors (1026.36(e)(3)).","Qualification requirements: licensing/registration, background and training for LOs at non-licensed employers (1026.36(f)).","NMLS ID of the individual and the organization on the credit application, note, security instrument and LE/CD (1026.36(g)).","Mandatory arbitration clauses and waivers of federal claims are prohibited (1026.36(h)); single-premium credit insurance may not be financed (1026.36(i)).","Records of compensation agreements and amounts paid must be kept 3 years (1026.25(c)(2)).","Bonuses from profits are capped at 10 percent of total compensation unless from a tax-advantaged plan (1026.36(d)(1)(iv))."],broker:"Steven must have a written lender-paid compensation election on file with each wholesale lender and may only change it on a prospective, documented schedule. On borrower-paid files he cannot also receive lender credits as compensation. When his LO (or he as owner-LO) pays for a borrower's cost overrun it must fit the unforeseen-cost exception. He should keep a three-option comparison on any file where steering could be questioned and ensure NMLS IDs are on the 1003, note and deed of trust. Any assistant or processor who quotes rates needs to be licensed.",penalties:"TILA damages (actual plus statutory, with the 1026.36 special rule allowing recovery of all finance charges and fees paid in the first 3 years for 36(d)/(e) violations), 3-year statute of limitations, CFPB penalties, license discipline; comp-plan violations are a frequent exam finding.",eff:"Fed rule effective April 2011; Dodd-Frank version effective Jan 10, 2014; NMLS ID requirement effective Jan 2014.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1026.36 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026/subpart-E/section-1026.36"},{t:"CFPB LO comp small entity guide",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/loan-originator-compensation-requirements/"}],verify:false},
{k:"hoepa",n:"Home Ownership and Equity Protection Act (High-Cost Mortgages)",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1639; 12 CFR 1026.32, 1026.34",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination"],summary:"HOEPA targets high-cost mortgages: loans whose APR, points and fees, or prepayment penalty exceed set triggers. A high-cost mortgage must come with special disclosures 3 business days before closing, pre-loan counseling, and a long list of banned terms (balloons, prepayment penalties, negative amortization, loans without regard to repayment ability). Few prime loans trip the triggers, but small loans and heavily priced non-QM loans can. Most wholesale lenders refuse to fund HOEPA loans at all, so the practical rule for a broker is to test every file and stay under the thresholds.",applies:"Consumer credit secured by the consumer's principal dwelling, including purchase loans, refinances, closed-end seconds and HELOCs; excludes reverse mortgages, construction-only loans and loans by Housing Finance Agencies.",key:["APR trigger: more than 6.5 points over APOR for first liens (8.5 if the dwelling is personal property and loan under $50,000), 8.5 points for subordinate liens (1026.32(a)(1)(i)).","Points-and-fees trigger: more than 5 percent of the total loan amount for loans of $26,968 or more in 2025 (2026 amount adjusts; verify), or 8 percent / $1,348 for smaller loans (1026.32(a)(1)(ii)).","Prepayment penalty trigger: any penalty more than 36 months after consummation or exceeding 2 percent of the prepaid amount (1026.32(a)(1)(iii)).","Points and fees include all finance charges except interest, all compensation paid to the LO, real estate fees not bona fide and reasonable, and prepayment penalties on refinanced loans (1026.32(b)).","Special disclosure 3 business days before consummation: APR, regular payment, loan amount, variable-rate statement, warning that the home may be lost (1026.32(c)).","Pre-loan counseling from a HUD-approved counselor, certified after the LE is received (1026.34(a)(5)).","Prohibited terms: balloon payments (with narrow exceptions), negative amortization, prepayment penalties, default interest, more than 2 periodic payments paid in advance, due-on-demand clauses (1026.32(d)).","No points and fees may be financed; no refinancing of a high-cost loan into another high-cost loan within 1 year without borrower benefit (1026.34(a)(3)-(4)).","Late fees capped at 4 percent and payoff statement fees limited (1026.34(a)(8)-(9)).","Assignee liability: anyone who buys a HOEPA loan is subject to all claims the borrower has against the originator (15 U.S.C. 1641(d))."],broker:"Steven should run the HOEPA points-and-fees and APR tests on every quote, especially small loans in IL and AZ and closed-end seconds in CA, because broker compensation counts toward points and fees. If a loan tests as high-cost he should restructure (lower comp, lender credits, different product) rather than proceed, since his lenders will decline it. He must also watch California's own high-cost law (Fin. Code 4970) and Illinois' High Risk Home Loan Act, which have different triggers.",penalties:"All TILA remedies plus recovery of all finance charges and fees paid; rescission up to 3 years; assignee liability; CFPB and state penalties; many states treat HOEPA violations as license-threatening.",eff:"Enacted 1994; Dodd-Frank expansion effective Jan 10, 2014; dollar triggers adjust each Jan 1.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1026.32 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026/subpart-E/section-1026.32"},{t:"CFPB HOEPA resources",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/hoepa/"}],verify:true},
{k:"glba_privacy",n:"Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act Privacy Rule / Regulation P",cite:"15 U.S.C. 6801-6809; 12 CFR Part 1016; 16 CFR Part 313",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["privacy","data-security"],summary:"GLBA requires financial institutions, including mortgage brokers, to tell consumers how they collect, share and protect nonpublic personal information (NPI) and to give them a chance to opt out of sharing with nonaffiliated third parties. The privacy notice must be delivered at the start of the customer relationship and annually unless an exception applies. Sharing for processing the transaction, with service providers under contract, or for fraud prevention does not require an opt-out. The FTC Safeguards Rule (separate entry) is the data-security half of GLBA.",applies:"Any business significantly engaged in financial activities, which includes mortgage brokers, lenders, servicers, real estate settlement services and appraisal management; NPI of consumers and customers.",key:["Initial privacy notice to customers no later than when the relationship is established (1016.4); consumers who only apply get it before NPI is shared outside the exceptions.","Annual notice unless the institution shares only under exceptions and has not changed its policies (1016.5(e), FAST Act exception).","Model privacy form in Appendix A provides a safe harbor.","Opt-out notice and reasonable means to opt out (30-day window) before sharing with nonaffiliated third parties outside exceptions (1016.7, 1016.10).","Exceptions: service providers and joint marketing under contract (1016.13); processing and servicing the transaction (1016.14); legal, fraud, consumer-consented sharing (1016.15).","No sharing of account numbers for marketing (1016.12).","Reuse and redisclosure limits on information received from another institution (1016.11).","Notice may be delivered electronically with E-SIGN consent or by posting with a reasonable notice.","State laws that give more protection (e.g., California Financial Information Privacy Act, Fin. Code 4050) are not preempted (15 U.S.C. 6807).","Mortgage brokers in California must also honor the CCPA/CPRA for data not covered by GLBA (see reglib2 ca_ccpa)."],broker:"Steven should deliver a GLBA privacy notice (model form) with every application package and keep proof. He should decide whether Patriot Pacific shares NPI outside the exceptions (most small brokers do not) so he can use the no-annual-notice exception. Vendor contracts (CRM, document portals, credit, processors) need confidentiality and reuse limits. He must never send a borrower's SSN or bank statements to a referral partner, and referral of a borrower to an insurance or real estate affiliate with NPI needs an opt-out or consent in California under Fin. Code 4052.",penalties:"FTC/CFPB enforcement with civil penalties (about $53,000 per violation under the FTC Act, indexed); state AG actions; no private right of action under GLBA but state laws (CA Fin. Code 4057: $2,500 per violation, up to $500,000) provide one.",eff:"Enacted 1999; privacy rule effective July 1, 2001; Reg P to CFPB 2011; annual notice exception added 2015.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1016 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1016"},{t:"FTC GLBA guidance",u:"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/privacy-security/gramm-leach-bliley-act"}],verify:false},
{k:"ftc_safeguards",n:"FTC Safeguards Rule (GLBA data security) with 2023 breach notification",cite:"16 CFR Part 314",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FTC",topic:["data-security","privacy"],summary:"The Safeguards Rule requires nonbank financial institutions, expressly including mortgage brokers, to maintain a written information security program with specific technical controls. The 2021 amendments (effective June 2023) added mandatory elements: a qualified individual in charge, risk assessments, encryption, multifactor authentication, access controls, vendor oversight, incident response and annual board reporting. A 2023 amendment added a 30-day FTC breach notification for events affecting 500 or more consumers. Small businesses with fewer than 5,000 consumers are exempt from a few written elements but not from the core controls.",applies:"Mortgage brokers, lenders, servicers, non-bank finance companies, real estate settlement services, tax preparers and other FTC-jurisdiction financial institutions; mortgage brokers are named in 314.2(h).",key:["Designate a qualified individual to oversee the program (314.4(a)).","Written risk assessment identifying internal and external risks, updated periodically (314.4(b)).","Access controls limiting who can see customer information; inventory of data, systems and devices (314.4(c)(1)-(2)).","Encrypt customer information in transit and at rest (314.4(c)(3)).","Multifactor authentication for anyone accessing an information system containing customer information (314.4(c)(5)).","Secure disposal of customer information no later than 2 years after last use unless needed (314.4(c)(6)).","Change management procedures and logging/monitoring of authorized user activity (314.4(c)(7)-(8)).","Continuous monitoring or annual penetration testing plus semiannual vulnerability assessments (314.4(d)(2)).","Staff security awareness training; oversee service providers by contract and periodic assessment (314.4(e)-(f)).","Written incident response plan (314.4(h)); notify the FTC within 30 days of discovering a notification event involving 500+ consumers (314.4(j), effective May 13, 2024).","Annual written report to the board or senior officer (314.4(i)); exemptions for institutions with information on fewer than 5,000 consumers from 314.4(b)(1), (d)(2), (h) and (i)."],broker:"Steven needs a written information security program naming himself or an IT provider as the qualified individual, MFA on email, LOS, CRM and cloud storage, full-disk encryption on laptops and phones, and a vendor list with security terms. He should purge old loan files (2-year rule after last use unless state retention requires longer, which it does for closed loans) and keep an incident response plan that includes the 30-day FTC notice and California's breach law (Civ. Code 1798.82). Any AI assistant or automation that touches borrower documents is a service provider that needs a contract and oversight.",penalties:"FTC Act Section 5 enforcement; civil penalties up to about $53,000 per violation per day for order violations; consent orders with 20-year monitoring; state AG and private claims under state breach laws.",eff:"Original rule 2003; amended rule effective Jan 10, 2022 with most controls effective June 9, 2023; breach notification effective May 13, 2024.",src:[{t:"16 CFR 314 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-314"},{t:"FTC Safeguards Rule guide for business",u:"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftc-safeguards-rule-what-your-business-needs-know"}],verify:false},
{k:"ftc_map",n:"FTC Mortgage Acts and Practices - Advertising Rule (Regulation N)",cite:"12 CFR Part 1014 (formerly 16 CFR 321)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["advertising"],summary:"Regulation N prohibits any material misrepresentation in a commercial communication about a mortgage credit product. It lists 19 categories of misrepresentation, from the interest rate and fees to government affiliation, the right to reside in the home, and the availability of counseling. It applies to brokers, lenders, lead generators and anyone else who advertises mortgages, including through social media, texts and AI-generated content. It is enforced by the CFPB, the FTC and state attorneys general, and it requires that advertising materials be kept for 2 years.",applies:"Any person that makes a commercial communication regarding a mortgage credit product, including brokers, lenders, servicers, lead generators, real estate professionals and marketing vendors; banks are excluded (covered by other rules).",key:["Prohibited misrepresentations include: the nature of the product (fixed vs. adjustable), interest rate, APR, payment amount, fees, existence or amount of a prepayment penalty, taxes and insurance, variability of terms (1014.3(a)-(h)).","Misrepresenting government affiliation, endorsement or sponsorship (e.g., FHA, VA, HUD seals or 'government program' language) (1014.3(k)).","Misrepresenting the consumer's ability to obtain a loan, pre-approval status, or that a loan has been approved (1014.3(l)-(m), (q)).","Misrepresenting the source of the communication (fake lender letterhead, 'your servicer' mailers) (1014.3(n)).","Misrepresenting the right to reside in the home, the right to a refund, counseling availability, or the availability of a reverse mortgage without repayment (1014.3(o)-(s)).","Material misrepresentations about savings, 'no closing costs', 'skip two payments' and similar claims are specifically called out in the FTC commentary.","Record retention: all commercial communications, substantiation and marketing materials kept 24 months from last dissemination (1014.5).","Applies to all media: direct mail, radio, TV, online, social, text, chat and automated voice.","State attorneys general may enforce Reg N (12 U.S.C. 5538)."],broker:"Steven should treat every Instagram post, text blast, flyer, radio spot or AI-written email about rates or programs as a Reg N communication: no 'government backed' language unless it is a true FHA/VA/USDA loan, no 'pre-approved' mailers, no payment examples that omit taxes/insurance or the rate assumption, and no suggesting he is the borrower's current servicer. He needs a marketing folder that keeps every ad and its substantiation for 2 years. VA-focused marketing to the Nellis AFB and San Diego military communities must not imply VA or DoD endorsement.",penalties:"CFPB and FTC civil penalties; state AG suits; coordinated enforcement sweeps (e.g., 2022-2024 actions against VA-marketing lenders); CA DRE and DFPI advertising discipline.",eff:"FTC MAP rule effective Aug 19, 2011; recodified as CFPB Regulation N Dec 30, 2011.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1014 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1014"},{t:"CFPB Regulation N page",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1014/"}],verify:false},
{k:"reg_n",n:"Regulation N quick reference (MAP Rule) - see ftc_map",cite:"12 CFR Part 1014",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["advertising"],summary:"Regulation N is the CFPB codification of the FTC Mortgage Acts and Practices Advertising Rule. It bans material misrepresentations in mortgage advertising and requires 2-year retention of ads and substantiation. This short entry exists so the dashboard can link the term Reg N directly; the full treatment is under ftc_map. The companion Regulation O (MARS) covers mortgage relief services.",applies:"Anyone advertising mortgage credit products other than banks and credit unions.",key:["Nineteen enumerated misrepresentation categories (1014.3).","Covers all commercial communications in any medium.","24-month retention of commercial communications and substantiation (1014.5).","Enforced by CFPB, FTC and state AGs.","No private right of action, but state UDAP laws often borrow its standards.","Rate and payment ads must also satisfy Reg Z 1026.24 trigger-term rules.","Government-affiliation claims are the most common VA/FHA marketing violation.","AI-generated marketing is covered the same as human-written."],broker:"Steven should route all marketing through a short Reg N checklist before it goes out and store copies for 2 years.",penalties:"Same as ftc_map.",eff:"Dec 30, 2011 (CFPB recodification).",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1014 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1014"}],verify:false},
{k:"reg_o",n:"Mortgage Assistance Relief Services Rule (Regulation O / MARS)",cite:"12 CFR Part 1015",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["advertising","servicing"],summary:"Regulation O regulates anyone who offers, for a fee, to help a homeowner get a loan modification, forbearance, short sale, deed in lieu or other relief from a mortgage. It bans advance fees until the consumer has a written offer from the servicer and accepts it, requires specific disclosures in ads and at the time of the offer, and prohibits telling a homeowner to stop communicating with or paying the servicer. Licensed attorneys have a limited exemption. Brokers and real estate agents who dabble in short-sale negotiation or 'loan mod help' for a fee are squarely covered.",applies:"Any person providing mortgage assistance relief services for consideration; exempts the servicer itself and attorneys meeting the 1015.7 conditions.",key:["No advance fee: payment may be collected only after the consumer executes a written agreement with the servicer incorporating the relief obtained (1015.5).","Disclosure in all general commercial communications: the provider is not associated with the government and the lender may not agree to change the loan (1015.4(a)).","Consumer-specific disclosures: the consumer may stop at any time without paying, the total fee, and that the lender may not agree (1015.4(b)).","When presenting an offer, give the servicer's written offer and a notice that the consumer may reject it without paying (1015.4(c)).","Prohibited representations: success rates, government affiliation, that the consumer should stop paying or stop talking to the servicer, or that the service is a counseling service (1015.3).","Attorney exemption only if engaged in the practice of law, licensed in the state, complies with state rules and (for advance fees) deposits them in a client trust account (1015.7).","Record retention 24 months for ads, contracts and consumer communications (1015.9).","Substantial assistance liability for anyone who knowingly helps a violator (1015.6).","California layers Civ. Code 2944.6-2944.7 (no advance fees for loan modification services; disclosure) on top of MARS."],broker:"Steven should not charge any fee for helping a client negotiate a modification, forbearance or short sale unless the engagement is structured strictly under Reg O and California Civ. Code 2944.7, which in practice means no fee at all before a signed servicer agreement. A short-sale listing commission paid by the lender at closing is permitted, but marketing that says 'we stop foreclosures' needs the Reg O disclosures. If he refers distressed borrowers to a HUD counselor or attorney he should document that no fee changed hands.",penalties:"FTC Act and CFPA penalties; consumer restitution; state criminal exposure under CA Civ. Code 2944.7 (misdemeanor, up to $10,000 fine and 1 year) and comparable NV/IL/AZ/FL foreclosure-consultant laws.",eff:"FTC MARS rule effective Dec 29, 2010 (advance-fee ban Jan 31, 2011); recodified as Reg O Dec 30, 2011.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1015 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1015"},{t:"FTC MARS guidance",u:"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/mortgage-assistance-relief-services-rule-compliance-guide-business"}],verify:false},
{k:"tcpa",n:"Telephone Consumer Protection Act and FCC rules",cite:"47 U.S.C. 227; 47 CFR 64.1200",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FCC (rules) / private plaintiffs and state AGs",topic:["telemarketing","advertising"],summary:"The TCPA restricts calls and texts made with an automatic telephone dialing system or an artificial or prerecorded voice, and marketing calls to numbers on the National Do Not Call Registry. Prior express written consent is required for marketing robocalls and texts to cell phones, and any reasonable method of revocation must be honored. The FCC confirmed in February 2024 that AI-generated voices are artificial voices. The one-to-one consent rule for lead generation was vacated by the Eleventh Circuit in January 2025, and the cross-channel 'revoke all' provision is delayed until January 31, 2027. The TCPA is the single largest class-action risk for mortgage marketing.",applies:"Anyone making calls or texts to consumers, including brokers, their assistants, dialer vendors, lead sellers and AI calling tools; the seller can be vicariously liable for its vendors.",key:["Prior express written consent for any marketing call or text to a cell phone using an autodialer or prerecorded/AI voice; the consent must name the seller and disclose that consent is not a condition of purchase (47 CFR 64.1200(a)(2), (f)(9)).","Artificial or prerecorded voice (including AI-cloned voice) calls to cell phones require consent even if not marketing; to landlines marketing requires consent (FCC Declaratory Ruling Feb 8, 2024).","National Do Not Call Registry: no telephone solicitations to registered numbers absent an established business relationship (18 months after a purchase, 3 months after an inquiry) or signed written consent (64.1200(c)(2)).","Company-specific do-not-call list and written DNC policy, with training (64.1200(d)).","Calling hours 8 a.m. to 9 p.m. local time of the called party (64.1200(c)(1)); some states are tighter (FL 8 p.m.).","Caller ID must be transmitted and must not be spoofed (64.1601(e); Truth in Caller ID Act).","Revocation of consent: consumer may revoke by any reasonable means (STOP, QUIT, END, etc.) and the caller must honor it within 10 business days (64.1200(a)(10), effective April 11, 2025); the 'revoke all' cross-channel provision is delayed to Jan 31, 2027.","Texts are calls under the TCPA (Facebook v. Duguid, 2021 narrowed the autodialer definition but prerecorded/AI and DNC rules still apply).","Reassigned numbers database safe harbor (64.1200(m)).","Statutory damages $500 per call/text, $1,500 if willful; no cap; 4-year statute of limitations.","Lead generation: the vacated one-to-one rule means multi-seller consent forms are again lawful federally, but consent must still clearly identify the seller(s) and many states (FL, WA, OK) have stricter mini-TCPA laws."],broker:"Steven should scrub every outbound list against the national DNC registry and his internal list, keep written DNC policy and training records, and collect prior express written consent (with Patriot Pacific named) on every web form before any text or auto-dialed call. He must honor opt-outs across all channels within 10 business days and preferably immediately. Any AI voice agent or ringless voicemail is an artificial voice and needs consent. Lead vendors should provide consent proof (TrustedForm or similar) and he should verify it before dialing. Florida leads need FTSA-compliant consent and 8 p.m. cutoff.",penalties:"$500 to $1,500 per call or text with no cap; class actions routinely settle in the millions; FCC forfeitures; state AG actions; FTC Telemarketing Sales Rule penalties (about $53,000 per violation).",eff:"Enacted 1991; 2012 written-consent rule effective Oct 16, 2013; AI voice ruling Feb 8, 2024; one-to-one rule vacated Jan 24, 2025; revocation rule effective April 11, 2025 (revoke-all portion delayed to Jan 31, 2027 by DA 26-12, Jan 6, 2026).",src:[{t:"47 CFR 64.1200 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-64/subpart-L/section-64.1200"},{t:"FCC AI voice declaratory ruling (Hunton summary)",u:"https://huntonak.com/privacy-and-information-security-law/fcc-issues-declaratory-ruling-that-tcpa-applies-to-ai-generated-voice-calls"},{t:"Eleventh Circuit vacates one-to-one consent (MoFo)",u:"https://www.mofo.com/resources/insights/250130-eleventh-circuit-vacates-fcc-s-tcpa-one-to-one-consent-rule"},{t:"FCC delays revoke-all to Jan 31, 2027 (Burr)",u:"https://www.burr.com/telephone-consumer-protection-act/the-fcc-delays-effective-date-of-tcpa-revoke-all-rule-until-january-31-2027"}],verify:false},
{k:"udaap",n:"Unfair, Deceptive, or Abusive Acts or Practices (UDAAP) and FTC Act Section 5",cite:"12 U.S.C. 5531, 5536; 15 U.S.C. 45",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC / state AGs",topic:["advertising","origination","servicing","fair-lending"],summary:"UDAAP is the catch-all standard that lets the CFPB and state attorneys general act against conduct that is unfair, deceptive or abusive even when no specific rule is broken. Unfair means substantial injury not reasonably avoidable and not outweighed by benefits; deceptive means a representation or omission likely to mislead a reasonable consumer on a material point; abusive means exploiting a consumer's lack of understanding or inability to protect their interests. Mortgage enforcement under UDAAP has covered junk fees, misleading rate ads, fake pre-approvals, steering veterans into costly refinances, and failing to honor rate locks. Every state has a parallel UDAP statute (CA B&P 17200/17500, FL FDUTPA, IL ICFA, NV NRS 598, AZ ARS 44-1522).",applies:"Any covered person or service provider offering consumer financial products, including mortgage brokers; FTC Act applies to all non-bank businesses.",key:["Deception test: representation, omission or practice likely to mislead; viewed from the perspective of a reasonable consumer; material (CFPB Exam Manual UDAAP).","Unfairness test: substantial injury, not reasonably avoidable, not outweighed by countervailing benefits (12 U.S.C. 5531(c)).","Abusiveness: materially interfering with understanding, or taking unreasonable advantage of lack of understanding, inability to protect interests, or reasonable reliance on the covered person (5531(d); CFPB Policy Statement April 2023).","Fine print and disclaimers do not cure a misleading headline claim; the net impression controls.","Charging fees for services not performed or inflating third-party fees is both a UDAAP and RESPA Section 8 issue.","Failing to honor a rate lock, or bait-and-switch on program or rate, is a classic deceptive practice.","Discrimination can be an unfair practice (CFPB 2022 position was vacated by a Texas court in Sept 2024 and the CFPB withdrew it in 2025; ECOA and FHA still apply).","Service-provider liability: brokers are responsible for marketing vendors and lead generators acting on their behalf.","State AGs may enforce federal UDAAP against nonbanks (12 U.S.C. 5552).","Record of substantiation for every advertising claim is the best defense."],broker:"Steven should write marketing in plain terms and be able to substantiate every claim (rate, savings, speed, 'no cost'). He should honor rate locks as written, disclose when a lock expires and who pays for extensions, and never charge a fee that does not match a real service. Special care applies to veteran and senior marketing, where 'abusive' findings are most common. He should review vendor scripts and AI chat outputs for claims he would not make himself.",penalties:"CFPB civil penalties in three tiers (indexed, roughly $7,000 / $35,000 / $1.4 million per day), restitution and disgorgement; FTC Section 5 penalties (about $53,000 per violation for rule or order violations); state UDAP statutes add per-violation penalties ($2,500 per violation in CA, $10,000 in FL) and private actions.",eff:"FTC Act 1914 (Section 5 as amended 1938); CFPA UDAAP effective July 21, 2011; CFPB abusiveness policy statement April 2023.",src:[{t:"CFPB UDAAP exam manual",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/supervision-examinations/unfair-deceptive-or-abusive-acts-or-practices-udaaps-examination-procedures/"},{t:"12 U.S.C. 5531 (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/12/5531"}],verify:false},
{k:"fha_hud",n:"FHA Single Family Mortgage Insurance Program (HUD Handbook 4000.1)",cite:"12 U.S.C. 1709 (National Housing Act Sec. 203); 24 CFR Parts 203, 202; HUD Handbook 4000.1",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"HUD / FHA",topic:["origination","military","appraisal"],summary:"FHA insures lenders against loss on low-down-payment mortgages originated under HUD rules. Brokers participate through FHA-approved sponsoring lenders (third-party originators) and must follow Handbook 4000.1 on borrower eligibility, credit, property standards and fees. FHA loans carry an upfront mortgage insurance premium and an annual premium, and limits are set by county each year. In 2025 HUD removed non-permanent residents from eligibility and has adjusted 203(k), appraisal and manual underwriting policies; 2026 limits run from $541,287 to $1,249,125 for one unit.",applies:"FHA-approved mortgagees and the brokers they sponsor; borrowers who will occupy a 1-4 unit property as a principal residence (with limited exceptions); property must meet HUD minimum property requirements.",key:["Minimum down payment 3.5 percent with credit score 580 or higher; 10 percent for 500-579; under 500 ineligible (4000.1 II.A.2).","Upfront MIP 1.75 percent of base loan amount (financeable); annual MIP 0.55 percent for most 30-year loans with LTV above 95 percent, 0.50 percent at 95 or less (ML 2023-05); MIP lasts the loan term if LTV above 90 percent at origination, 11 years otherwise.","2026 loan limits: floor $541,287, ceiling $1,249,125 (one unit), effective for case numbers assigned on or after Jan 1, 2026 (ML 2025-23); San Diego County sits at the ceiling (verify county figure).","Non-permanent residents ineligible for case numbers on or after May 25, 2025 (ML 2025-09); lawful permanent residents remain eligible.","Maximum DTI with AUS approval typically up to 56.99 percent; manual underwriting 31/43 base, up to 40/50 with compensating factors (4000.1 II.A.5).","Seller concessions capped at 6 percent of the sales price; buyer's agent commission paid by seller is not counted as a concession (ML 2024-?? verify) and may be paid by the borrower.","Property must meet Minimum Property Requirements and Standards; FHA appraisal valid 180 days; FHA roster appraiser required; required repairs and the 90-day flip rule (24 CFR 203.37a).","203(k) limited program cap raised to $75,000 and extended timelines (ML 2024-13, effective Nov 4, 2024).","Streamline refinance: net tangible benefit test, 210-day seasoning and 6 payments; cash-out LTV cap 80 percent (ML 2019-11).","Self-sufficiency test for 3-4 units; rental income rules; identity-of-interest 85 percent LTV limits; gift funds fully allowed with documentation.","Sponsoring lender responsibility: TPO brokers need no FHA approval but the sponsor's Quality Control plan covers them (4000.1 I.A.5)."],broker:"Steven can broker FHA through his sponsored wholesale lenders without his own FHA approval, but he must know Handbook 4000.1 well enough to pre-qualify correctly (credit tiers, DTI, residency status, property condition). He should check the county limit for every FHA file (San Diego, Riverside, Orange, LA, Clark, Maricopa, Cook, Miami-Dade all differ) and quote MIP correctly. He must not advertise FHA as a government loan 'for everyone' or imply HUD endorsement. For 2-4 unit purchases he should run the self-sufficiency test early.",penalties:"Indemnification and repurchase demands on the sponsoring lender, Mortgagee Review Board sanctions, False Claims Act exposure for knowingly false certifications (treble damages), and removal of the broker from the sponsor's approved list.",eff:"National Housing Act 1934; Handbook 4000.1 consolidated Sept 14, 2015 with continuous updates; 2026 limits effective Jan 1, 2026.",src:[{t:"HUD Handbook 4000.1",u:"https://www.hud.gov/program_offices/administration/hudclips/handbooks/hsgh/4000.1"},{t:"ML 2025-09 residency requirements",u:"https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/OCHCO/documents/2025-09hsgml.pdf"},{t:"HUD FHA lender page / 2026 limits",u:"https://www.hud.gov/hud-partners/single-family-lender"}],verify:true},
{k:"bsa_aml",n:"Bank Secrecy Act / Anti-Money Laundering Program for Non-Bank Residential Mortgage Lenders and Originators",cite:"31 U.S.C. 5318(h); 31 CFR 1029 (RMLOs)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FinCEN",topic:["data-security","origination"],summary:"Since 2012 FinCEN has required non-bank residential mortgage lenders and originators, including mortgage brokers, to maintain a written anti-money-laundering program and to file Suspicious Activity Reports. The program must be risk-based and include policies, a compliance officer, training and independent testing. Brokers file SARs for suspected mortgage fraud, identity theft and money laundering of $5,000 or more. FinCEN's separate 2024 rule requiring reports on non-financed residential real estate transfers to entities and trusts was vacated by a federal court in March 2026, and domestic beneficial ownership reporting was eliminated in March 2025.",applies:"Any person that accepts a residential mortgage loan application or offers or negotiates terms (RMLO definition, 31 CFR 1029.100(m)); includes brokers of any size.",key:["Written AML program with four pillars: internal policies and controls, designated compliance officer, ongoing training, independent testing (1029.210).","Program must be approved by senior management and available to FinCEN on request.","SAR filing within 30 days of detecting a suspicious transaction of $5,000 or more (60 days if no suspect identified) (1029.320); confidentiality of SARs is mandatory.","Typical SAR triggers: income or asset misrepresentation, straw buyers, altered bank statements, occupancy fraud, identity theft, unusual gift funds, undisclosed debts.","Record retention 5 years for SARs and supporting documentation.","Currency transaction report (Form 8300 / CTR) for cash over $10,000 (rare for brokers).","Section 314(b) voluntary information sharing registration available.","Residential Real Estate Transfer Rule (31 CFR 1031.320, non-financed transfers to entities/trusts) was set for March 1, 2026 but vacated nationwide on March 19, 2026 by a Texas district court; Geographic Targeting Orders for title companies continue.","Corporate Transparency Act BOI reporting: domestic companies exempt under the March 26, 2025 interim final rule; foreign reporting companies still file.","OFAC screening is a separate obligation (see ofac entry) but usually built into the AML program."],broker:"Steven needs a written AML program for Patriot Pacific naming himself (or a designated officer) as AML compliance officer, annual training documentation for himself and any staff, and an independent test at least every 12-18 months (can be an outside consultant). He must file SARs through BSA E-Filing when he sees mortgage fraud red flags and never tell the borrower a SAR was filed. He does not need to file FinCEN real estate reports after the March 2026 vacatur, but he should keep an eye on whether FinCEN re-proposes the rule. Patriot Pacific as a domestic entity does not file a BOI report under current rules.",penalties:"Civil penalties up to about $25,000 per day for program failures (indexed), per-violation SAR penalties, criminal penalties for willful violations (up to $250,000 and 5 years), and state license consequences.",eff:"RMLO AML rule effective Aug 13, 2012; BOI domestic exemption March 26, 2025; RRE transfer rule vacated March 19, 2026.",src:[{t:"31 CFR 1029 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-31/subtitle-B/chapter-X/part-1029"},{t:"FinCEN BOI page",u:"https://www.fincen.gov/BOI"},{t:"Gibson Dunn: FinCEN RRE rule vacated nationwide",u:"https://www.gibsondunn.com/fincen-residential-real-estate-reporting-rule-vacated-nationwide/"}],verify:false},
{k:"flood_nfip",n:"National Flood Insurance Program and Mandatory Purchase Requirement",cite:"42 U.S.C. 4001 et seq.; 42 U.S.C. 4012a; 12 CFR Parts 22, 339, 614, 760 (lender rules); 24 CFR 203.16a (FHA)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FEMA (NFIP) / federal banking agencies / HUD, VA, GSEs",topic:["closing","servicing"],summary:"Federal law requires flood insurance on any loan secured by a building in a Special Flood Hazard Area (zones A and V) if the lender is federally regulated or the loan is sold to Fannie, Freddie, FHA, VA or USDA. The lender must order a flood determination, give the borrower notice before closing, and force-place coverage if it lapses. The NFIP itself must be reauthorized by Congress and has run on short-term extensions since 2017; it is currently authorized through December 11, 2026. During a lapse, new NFIP policies cannot be written and closings in flood zones stall unless private flood coverage is used.",applies:"All loans secured by improved real property in an SFHA in NFIP-participating communities; private flood insurance must be accepted if it meets the statutory definition.",key:["Standard Flood Hazard Determination Form (SFHDF) required on every loan; determination may be relied on for 7 years if no map change (42 U.S.C. 4104b).","Notice to borrower of SFHA status and availability of coverage a reasonable time before closing (typically 10 days) (12 CFR 22.9).","Coverage amount: lesser of the outstanding principal, the insurable value (replacement cost) or the NFIP maximum ($250,000 residential building, $100,000 contents; $500,000 for non-residential).","Escrow of flood premiums required for loans made or modified after Jan 1, 2016 by lenders over $1 billion in assets (HFIAA).","Force-placement after 45-day notice; refund of overlapping premiums (42 U.S.C. 4012a(e)).","Private flood insurance must be accepted if it meets the definition; may be accepted under discretionary standards (2019 interagency rule).","FHA accepts private flood policies for case numbers on or after Dec 21, 2022 (ML 2022-18).","30-day waiting period for NFIP policies except at loan closing (no wait when purchased in connection with a loan).","Risk Rating 2.0 pricing (Oct 2021) ties premiums to replacement cost and distance to water, not just zone; premiums are capped at 18 percent annual increases.","NFIP authorization: lapsed Feb 1-3, 2026 and 43 days in late 2025; now authorized through Dec 11, 2026 (P.L. 119-103).","Condo and PUD: RCBAP master policy must cover 80 percent of replacement cost or the unit's share for GSE loans."],broker:"Steven should pull the flood zone early (free FEMA map lookup) on every property, especially coastal San Diego, Miami-Dade, and Las Vegas washes, so the premium goes into the DTI and the LE. He should quote NFIP and private flood options and warn buyers in SFHAs that a lapse before Dec 11, 2026 could delay closing unless a private policy is bound. He must make sure the lender's flood notice is delivered well before the CD. Condo buyers in FL need the association's master flood policy reviewed.",penalties:"Lender civil penalties up to about $2,000 per violation (indexed) with no annual cap for pattern violations; loans without required coverage are unsaleable; borrower uninsured losses.",eff:"NFIA 1968; FDPA 1973; Biggert-Waters 2012; HFIAA 2014; Risk Rating 2.0 Oct 2021; current authorization through Dec 11, 2026.",src:[{t:"FEMA NFIP reauthorization page",u:"https://www.fema.gov/flood-insurance/rules-legislation/congressional-reauthorization"},{t:"NFIP extended to Dec 11, 2026 (Proctor)",u:"https://proctorlp.com/compliance/nfip-extended-to-december-11-2026/"}],verify:false},
{k:"hpa_pmi",n:"Homeowners Protection Act (PMI cancellation)",cite:"12 U.S.C. 4901-4910",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["servicing","origination"],summary:"The HPA requires lenders to disclose PMI terms at closing and to cancel borrower-paid PMI automatically when the loan reaches 78 percent of the original value, or on borrower request at 80 percent, on single-family principal residences. It also requires annual notices of cancellation rights and a final notice when PMI ends. It does not apply to FHA MIP or VA funding fees, and lender-paid PMI has its own disclosure. The law matters to brokers when structuring down payments and when refinancing clients out of PMI.",applies:"Residential mortgage transactions on single-family principal residences closed on or after July 29, 1999 with borrower-paid PMI; high-risk loans have modified rules; FHA, VA and USDA are excluded.",key:["Borrower-requested cancellation at 80 percent LTV based on the original value (or amortization schedule), good payment history, no subordinate liens, and value not declined (12 U.S.C. 4902(a)).","Automatic termination at 78 percent LTV by the amortization schedule if the borrower is current (4902(b)).","Final termination at the midpoint of the amortization period if PMI is still in force (4902(c)).","Initial disclosures at consummation for fixed-rate and ARMs explaining cancellation rights (4903(a)).","Annual written statement of cancellation rights and servicer contact (4903(a)(3)).","Notice within 30 days of termination that PMI has ended and no further premiums are due (4903(b)).","Refund of unearned premiums within 45 days of cancellation (4902(f)).","Lender-paid PMI must be disclosed as such with a comparison to borrower-paid (4905).","High-risk loans (as defined by Fannie/Freddie) may keep PMI to 77 percent LTV or midpoint (4902(g)).","GSEs allow cancellation based on current value at 75-80 percent LTV after 2-5 years with a new appraisal/BPO (servicing guides)."],broker:"Steven should explain PMI cancellation rights when a client chooses 5-15 percent down on a conventional loan and compare to lender-paid PMI or an 80/10/10. When San Diego or Temecula clients have gained equity he can help them request cancellation using the GSE current-value rules rather than refinance unnecessarily (a UDAAP point as well). He should confirm the closing package has the HPA disclosure on every PMI loan.",penalties:"Actual damages, statutory damages up to $2,000 per individual (class actions capped at lesser of $500,000 or 1 percent of net worth), costs and fees; agency enforcement; 2-year statute of limitations.",eff:"Enacted July 29, 1998, effective July 29, 1999; amended 2000.",src:[{t:"12 U.S.C. 4901 et seq. (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/12/chapter-49"},{t:"CFPB HPA compliance bulletin 2015-03",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/supervisory-guidance/bulletin-private-mortgage-insurance-cancellation-and-termination/"}],verify:false},
{k:"scra",n:"Servicemembers Civil Relief Act",cite:"50 U.S.C. 3901-4043",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"DOJ (enforcement) / CFPB / HUD",topic:["military","servicing"],summary:"The SCRA gives active-duty servicemembers protections on obligations incurred before service: a 6 percent interest-rate cap on pre-service debts including mortgages (extended to one year after service for mortgages), protection from foreclosure without a court order during service and one year after, protection from default judgments, and lease termination rights. It is a servicing-side law but originators must understand it when a client is in or about to enter active duty, and VA loans carry additional VA servicing protections. DOJ has brought large cases against servicers for SCRA foreclosure and interest-cap violations.",applies:"Active-duty members of the Army, Navy, Air Force, Marines, Space Force, Coast Guard, activated Guard and Reserve, commissioned PHS and NOAA officers, and in some provisions their dependents; obligations that predate the period of military service.",key:["Interest cap: 6 percent on debts incurred before active duty, upon written notice and orders; for mortgages the cap runs through service plus 1 year (50 U.S.C. 3937); forgiven interest, not deferred.","Servicer must apply the cap retroactively to the date of orders once notice is received; notice may be given up to 180 days after release from service.","Foreclosure of a pre-service mortgage during service and for 1 year after requires a court order or a valid waiver (3953); non-judicial foreclosure (CA, NV, AZ) is barred without an order.","Default judgment protection: plaintiff must file an affidavit of military status; court may appoint counsel and stay proceedings (3931).","Stay of proceedings on request (3932); lease termination for PCS orders or deployment of 90+ days (3955).","Waivers of SCRA rights must be in writing, in a separate document, executed during or after service, in at least 12-point type (3918).","Residence and domicile protections against state taxation (3998-4001).","DMDC SCRA website is the standard tool to verify military status before any adverse action.","VA loans add VA servicer requirements (38 CFR 36.4300 et seq.) and the 2025 partial claim program.","Military Lending Act (separate entry) does not cover mortgages, but SCRA does."],broker:"Steven serves a heavy military base (San Diego Navy/Marine Corps, Nellis AFB) so he should ask about upcoming activation or deployment when structuring loans, explain that the 6 percent cap only applies to debts taken on before active duty (a new VA loan taken while on active duty is not capped), and keep a plain-language SCRA handout. For clients receiving PCS orders he should explain lease termination rights and VA occupancy exceptions. If a military client falls behind he should direct them to the servicer's SCRA desk and the VA loan technician line, and remind them that a non-judicial foreclosure during service is void.",penalties:"DOJ civil actions with civil penalties (about $25,000 first violation, $75,000 subsequent, indexed) and damages to each servicemember; private right of action with damages and attorney fees (3931, 4042); foreclosures in violation are voidable; knowing violations are misdemeanors.",eff:"Enacted Dec 19, 2003 (replacing the 1940 Soldiers and Sailors Civil Relief Act); mortgage foreclosure protection period permanently set at 1 year post-service in 2018.",src:[{t:"50 U.S.C. Chapter 50 (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/50/chapter-50"},{t:"DOJ Servicemembers and Veterans Initiative",u:"https://www.justice.gov/servicemembers/servicemembers-civil-relief-act-scra"}],verify:false},
{k:"mla",n:"Military Lending Act and 32 CFR 232",cite:"10 U.S.C. 987; 32 CFR Part 232",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"DoD (rule) / CFPB, FTC and prudential regulators (enforcement)",topic:["military","origination"],summary:"The MLA caps the cost of consumer credit extended to active-duty servicemembers and their dependents at a 36 percent Military APR and bans mandatory arbitration, prepayment penalties and certain other terms. Residential mortgages, refinances, HELOCs and purchase-money loans secured by the dwelling are excluded, so a mortgage broker's loans are outside the MLA. It matters to Steven because unsecured 'bridge' or personal loans, credit cards and certain down-payment products marketed to military clients are covered, and because many lenders run an MLA database check on every file regardless.",applies:"Consumer credit to covered borrowers (active-duty members and dependents) other than residential mortgages, purchase-money loans secured by the item purchased, and certain other exemptions (32 CFR 232.3(f)).",key:["36 percent MAPR cap including most fees, credit insurance and add-on products (232.4).","Residential mortgage exclusion: loans secured by a dwelling including purchase, refinance, HELOC and reverse mortgages are not consumer credit under the MLA (232.3(f)(2)(i)).","Covered borrower status determined by the DoD MLA database or a consumer report with MLA indicator; safe harbor if checked (232.5).","Mandatory arbitration, waiver of legal rights, and prepayment penalties prohibited on covered loans (232.8).","Oral and written disclosure of the MAPR and payment obligation (232.6).","Applies to dependents (spouse, children under 21 or 23 if students) with military ID.","Violations make the contract void from inception (10 U.S.C. 987(f)(3)).","Not to be confused with the SCRA, which protects pre-service obligations and covers mortgages."],broker:"Steven's mortgages are exempt, but if he ever refers a military borrower to a personal loan, a debt-consolidation product, or a non-mortgage down-payment loan he should confirm the lender handles MLA. He should not let a marketing partner bundle a covered credit product with a mortgage pitch to military families. Running the MLA database check is a cheap safe-harbor habit for lenders he works with.",penalties:"Contracts void; civil liability including actual and punitive damages and attorney fees; criminal misdemeanor for knowing violations (up to 1 year); CFPB and FTC enforcement.",eff:"Enacted 2006; DoD rule 2007; expanded rule effective Oct 3, 2016 (credit cards Oct 3, 2017).",src:[{t:"32 CFR 232 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-32/subtitle-A/chapter-I/subchapter-M/part-232"},{t:"CFPB MLA resources",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/other-applicable-requirements/military-lending-act/"}],verify:false},
{k:"esign",n:"Electronic Signatures in Global and National Commerce Act (E-SIGN) and UETA",cite:"15 U.S.C. 7001-7031; Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (CA Civ. Code 1633.1 et seq.; NV NRS 719; AZ ARS 44-7001; FL Fla. Stat. 668.50; IL 815 ILCS 333 (2021 UETA))",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FTC / state law",topic:["closing","origination","privacy"],summary:"E-SIGN makes electronic signatures and records legally valid and sets the conditions for delivering consumer disclosures electronically: the consumer must affirmatively consent after receiving a disclosure of hardware and software requirements, must be able to demonstrate access, and may withdraw consent. For mortgage brokers this governs e-signed applications, LEs, CDs and disclosures and the timing rules that depend on receipt. Deeds of trust and notes can be e-signed (eNotes with MERS eRegistry) but recording and notarization depend on state law and county e-recording acceptance. Federal law coexists with each state's UETA.",applies:"Any transaction in or affecting interstate commerce where a signature or record is required; consumer disclosure consent rules apply when a law requires written information to a consumer.",key:["Electronic signatures and records may not be denied legal effect solely because they are electronic (15 U.S.C. 7001(a)).","Consumer consent: clear and conspicuous statement of right to paper, scope of consent, how to withdraw, hardware/software requirements, and consent given or confirmed electronically in a manner that reasonably demonstrates access (7001(c)).","TRID receipt rules: an electronically delivered LE or CD is received when the consumer acknowledges or 3 business days after sending if consent is in place (Reg Z Comment 19(e)(1)(iv)-2).","Records must be accurately retained and accessible to all parties for the required retention period (7001(d)).","Notices of foreclosure, default, repossession, eviction and the right to cure on a primary residence are excluded from E-SIGN and must be on paper (7003(b)(2)(B)).","Notarization: E-SIGN permits electronic notarization where state law allows (7001(g)); RON is governed by state law (NV, FL, AZ, IL allow RON; CA RON law enacted 2023 is not yet operative, see reglib2).","eNotes: transferable records under 7021 with a single authoritative copy and control (MERS eRegistry); Fannie, Freddie, FHA, VA accept eNotes.","Security instruments require county e-recording; most CA, NV, AZ, FL and IL counties accept e-recorded documents through trusted submitters.","Signature authentication and audit trails are the evidence of validity; tamper-evident seals recommended."],broker:"Steven should capture E-SIGN consent in his point-of-sale before sending any disclosure electronically and keep the consent record with the file. He should remember the 3-business-day mailbox rule still applies to e-delivered CDs unless the borrower actually opens and acknowledges them, so getting an acknowledgment moves closings. He can use e-signed 1003s and broker disclosures in all five states, but closings in California still typically use in-person or hybrid signings with a mobile notary because RON is not yet live there.",penalties:"No specific penalty; records and signatures without proper consent may be unenforceable or disclosures deemed undelivered, creating TILA/TRID exposure; state UETA remedies.",eff:"E-SIGN effective Oct 1, 2000 (record retention provisions March 1, 2001); UETA adopted in CA 2000, NV 2001, AZ 2000, FL 2000, IL 2021.",src:[{t:"15 U.S.C. 7001 (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/7001"},{t:"CA UETA Civ. Code 1633.1",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=2.5.&part=2.&chapter=&article="}],verify:false},
{k:"can_spam",n:"CAN-SPAM Act and FTC rule",cite:"15 U.S.C. 7701-7713; 16 CFR Part 316",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FTC / FCC (mobile)",topic:["advertising","telemarketing"],summary:"CAN-SPAM sets the rules for commercial email: no false headers or deceptive subject lines, a clear identification that the message is an ad, a valid physical postal address, and a working opt-out that is honored within 10 business days. It applies to every commercial email, including one-off emails from a loan officer to a prospect, and the sender is liable for vendors who send on its behalf. Transactional or relationship messages (status updates on an existing loan) are exempt from most requirements. State laws cannot add stricter email rules except for fraud, but California B&P 17529.5 adds its own penalties for deceptive email.",applies:"Any person who initiates or procures commercial electronic mail; mobile commercial messages to wireless domains require express prior authorization (FCC rule 47 CFR 64.3100).",key:["No false or misleading header information (From, To, routing) (15 U.S.C. 7704(a)(1)).","No deceptive subject lines (7704(a)(2)).","Identify the message as an advertisement in a clear and conspicuous way unless the recipient gave affirmative consent (7704(a)(5)(A)(i)).","Include a valid physical postal address (7704(a)(5)(A)(iii)).","Provide a clear opt-out mechanism that works for at least 30 days after sending (7704(a)(3)).","Honor opt-outs within 10 business days and do not sell or transfer opted-out addresses (7704(a)(4)).","Sender is responsible for messages sent by a vendor or affiliate on its behalf (7704(a)(1), 'procures').","Sexually explicit material labeling (not relevant) and prohibition on harvesting addresses or dictionary attacks (7704(b)).","Transactional/relationship messages are exempt from the opt-out and ad-identification rules but not the no-false-header rule (7702(17)).","Messages to wireless domains (SMS gateways) need express prior authorization under the FCC rule."],broker:"Steven's CRM campaigns, newsletter and even individual prospecting emails need his business address and an unsubscribe link, and his automation must suppress unsubscribed contacts within 10 business days. Subject lines like 'Your rate has been approved' are deceptive unless true. Emails about a borrower's active loan are transactional and do not need the opt-out, but mixed messages (status plus a refinance pitch) are commercial. He should check any AI-drafted email for header and subject accuracy before sending.",penalties:"FTC civil penalties up to about $53,000 per email (indexed) with no cap; FCC penalties; state AG and ISP suits; criminal penalties for fraud-type violations; CA B&P 17529.5: $1,000 per email up to $1 million per incident.",eff:"Effective Jan 1, 2004; FTC rule amendments 2008.",src:[{t:"FTC CAN-SPAM compliance guide",u:"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business"},{t:"16 CFR 316 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-316"}],verify:false},
{k:"fair_housing",n:"Fair Housing Act (Title VIII of the Civil Rights Act of 1968)",cite:"42 U.S.C. 3601-3619; 24 CFR Parts 100, 103, 110",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"HUD / DOJ",topic:["fair-lending","real-estate","advertising"],summary:"The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in the sale, rental, financing and advertising of housing on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, familial status and disability. For a mortgage broker it bars discriminatory loan pricing, terms, steering, redlining and advertising; for a real estate broker it bars steering, blockbusting and refusing to show or sell. HUD's 2013 disparate impact rule was reinstated in 2023, but HUD proposed removing it in January 2026 and supplemented that proposal in August 2026; the statute and Supreme Court precedent (Inclusive Communities, 2015) still recognize disparate impact claims. The Equal Housing Opportunity logo or statement is expected in advertising.",applies:"Anyone engaged in residential real estate-related transactions including making or purchasing loans, appraising, selling, renting, advertising; exemptions for owner-occupied small buildings and single-family sales by owner without a broker do not cover lending.",key:["Section 804: unlawful to refuse to sell or rent, discriminate in terms, advertise a preference, misrepresent availability, or blockbust (42 U.S.C. 3604).","Section 805: discrimination in residential real estate-related transactions (loans, appraisals, insurance) (3605).","Section 806: discrimination in brokerage services (3606).","Protected classes: race, color, religion, sex (including sexual orientation and gender identity under HUD's 2021 memo), national origin, familial status, disability (3602).","Reasonable accommodations and modifications for persons with disabilities; design and construction accessibility standards for multifamily built after March 1991 (3604(f)).","Advertising: no words, phrases, photographs or symbols indicating a preference; HUD advertising guidelines; EHO logo and slogan (24 CFR 109 (rescinded) guidance still followed).","Disparate impact: HUD rule at 24 CFR 100.500 (2013, reinstated March 31, 2023); HUD NPRM Jan 14, 2026 to remove subpart G; supplemental NPRM Aug 10, 2026 (not final as of Oct 2026).","Redlining: refusing or limiting lending based on the demographics of a neighborhood; DOJ Combating Redlining Initiative actions 2021-2024.","Complaint to HUD within 1 year; civil suit within 2 years (3610, 3613); administrative law judge penalties.","State and local laws add classes (CA FEHA adds source of income, marital status, ancestry, veteran status and more)."],broker:"Steven must apply the same programs, pricing and effort to every client and every neighborhood in San Diego, Riverside, Orange and LA counties, and keep notes that show it. Marketing images and copy should not signal a preferred buyer; he should include the EHO logo on real estate and lending ads. As a real estate broker he may not steer buyers by school demographics or neighborhood composition. Appraisal bias complaints should be routed through the lender's ROV process. He should assume California FEHA and DFPI fair lending exams (AB 801, effective 2027) will apply disparate impact analysis whatever HUD does.",penalties:"HUD ALJ civil penalties (about $25,000 first violation, up to about $125,000 for repeat, indexed); DOJ pattern-or-practice suits with civil penalties over $100,000; private actions for actual and punitive damages and attorney fees; license discipline; CA FEHA damages uncapped.",eff:"Enacted April 11, 1968; amended 1988 (familial status, disability, enforcement); disparate impact rule 2013, 2020 (never effective), reinstated 2023; proposed rescission 2026 pending.",src:[{t:"42 U.S.C. 3601 et seq. (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/chapter-45/subchapter-I"},{t:"HUD proposes to remove disparate impact rule (Jan 2026)",u:"https://www.consumerfinancemonitor.com/2026/01/14/hud-proposes-to-remove-its-fair-housing-act-disparate-impact-rule/"}],verify:false},
{k:"va_lending",n:"VA Home Loan Guaranty Program (Lenders Handbook VA Pamphlet 26-7)",cite:"38 U.S.C. Chapter 37; 38 CFR Part 36; VA Pamphlet 26-7",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"Department of Veterans Affairs, Loan Guaranty Service",topic:["military","origination","appraisal","servicing"],summary:"VA guarantees a portion of loans made by private lenders to eligible veterans, servicemembers and surviving spouses, allowing 100 percent financing with no monthly mortgage insurance. Brokers originate VA loans through VA-approved automatic lenders (agents). Key rules cover eligibility and entitlement, the funding fee, residual income underwriting, the Notice of Value appraisal process, allowable fees and the 1 percent flat charge, and occupancy. 2024-2026 changes include the temporary variance allowing veterans to pay buyer-broker fees (Aug 2024), the end of VASP intake (May 2025), the VA Home Loan Program Reform Act creating a partial claim program (signed July 30, 2025; launched June 15, 2026), and the February 2026 announcement that the funding fee is deductible as mortgage insurance for tax year 2026 (verify).",applies:"VA-eligible borrowers (service requirements, COE) buying or refinancing a primary residence 1-4 units; lenders with VA automatic authority and their sponsored brokers.",key:["No down payment up to the county loan limit for borrowers with full entitlement; with partial entitlement, VA guarantees 25 percent of the county limit (2026 one-unit conforming limit $832,750 baseline, $1,249,125 ceiling) (Blue Water Navy Act 2019).","Funding fee: 2.15 percent first use, 3.3 percent subsequent use with less than 5 percent down; 1.5 percent with 5-10 percent down; 1.25 percent with 10 percent or more; IRRRL 0.5 percent; cash-out 2.15/3.3 percent; exempt if receiving VA disability compensation, Purple Heart recipient on active duty, or surviving spouse (38 U.S.C. 3729; rates through at least Nov 2031 (verify)).","Residual income tables by region and family size plus 41 percent DTI guideline; DTI over 41 needs residual income 120 percent of the table (26-7 Ch. 4).","Allowable fees: itemized list plus a 1 percent flat charge in lieu of all other lender fees; veteran may not pay broker fees except as allowed; termite inspection payable by veteran since 2022 (verify) (38 CFR 36.4313).","Buyer-broker commission: veteran may pay a reasonable and customary buyer-broker fee under Circular 26-24-14 (effective Aug 10, 2024) pending permanent rulemaking; cannot be financed.","Appraisal: VA fee panel appraiser, Notice of Value, Tidewater process, Minimum Property Requirements; reconsideration of value procedures.","Seller concessions capped at 4 percent of value (excluding normal closing costs paid by seller).","IRRRL: 210 days and 6 payments seasoning, net tangible benefit, 36-month recoupment of fees (38 U.S.C. 3709); cash-out refinance Type I/II rules and 100 percent LTV cap.","Occupancy within 60 days (up to 12 months with justification); spouse or dependent child occupancy allowed for deployed members.","Loss mitigation: VASP stopped accepting new submissions May 1, 2025; Partial Claim Program (up to a statutory percentage of UPB, 5-year authorization) launched June 15, 2026 with servicer implementation deadline Nov 28, 2026 (verify percentages).","Assumability with VA approval; entitlement restoration on payoff or substitution."],broker:"Steven's VA business (San Diego Navy/Marine, Nellis AFB) depends on getting the COE and entitlement math right at pre-approval, quoting the correct funding fee and exemption, and pricing with the 1 percent flat charge in mind. He should prepare sellers' agents for VA appraisal timelines and MPRs, use Tidewater and ROV when values come in low, and explain the buyer-broker fee rules since the NAR settlement. Every VA ad must avoid VA or DoD endorsement claims (Reg N). For struggling VA borrowers he should point to the new partial claim and the VA loan technician line (877-827-3702).",penalties:"VA sanctions on the automatic lender (indemnification, loss of authority), False Claims Act exposure for false certifications, and inability to sell the loan; broker loses access to the sponsor.",eff:"GI Bill 1944; Blue Water Navy Act loan-limit change Jan 1, 2020; Circular 26-24-14 Aug 10, 2024; VA Home Loan Program Reform Act July 30, 2025; partial claim program June 15, 2026.",src:[{t:"VA Lenders Handbook 26-7",u:"https://www.benefits.va.gov/warms/pam26_7.asp"},{t:"VA Circular 26-24-14 summary (Black Mann & Graham)",u:"https://bmandg.com/va-circular-26-24-14-creates-temporary-local-variance-for-veteran-buyers-to-pay-buyer-broker-charges/"},{t:"VA partial claim program coverage",u:"https://themortgagereports.com/133801/va-partial-claim-program"}],verify:true},
{k:"usda_rd",n:"USDA Rural Development Single Family Housing Guaranteed Loan Program (Section 502)",cite:"42 U.S.C. 1472; 7 CFR Part 3555; HB-1-3555",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"USDA Rural Development",topic:["origination"],summary:"USDA guarantees 100 percent LTV loans in eligible rural areas for low-to-moderate income households (up to 115 percent of area median income). The loan has an upfront guarantee fee and an annual fee instead of mortgage insurance, and the property must be in an eligible area on the USDA map. Brokers participate through USDA-approved lenders. Income limits are updated each year (2026 limits effective July 13, 2026 via PN 657), and USDA in July 2026 announced guarantee-fee reductions and proposed excluding real estate commissions from the 6 percent seller concession limit (verify details).",applies:"Households with adjusted income at or below the moderate income limit buying a primary residence in an eligible rural area; approved lenders and their TPOs.",key:["100 percent LTV plus the upfront guarantee fee may be financed (7 CFR 3555.107).","Upfront guarantee fee 1.00 percent and annual fee 0.35 percent for FY2024-FY2026 (USDA announced a reduction July 2026; verify new amounts and effective date).","Income limit: 115 percent of AMI by county, household size 1-4 and 5-8 tiers; all adult household income counts for eligibility even if not on the loan (3555.152).","Property must be in an eligible rural area per the USDA eligibility map; large parts of Riverside County (Menifee, Winchester, parts of Temecula outskirts), Clark County outskirts and Arizona exurbs qualify; San Diego County has limited eligible areas.","GUS automated underwriting; manual underwriting ratios 29/41 with compensating factors for higher (3555.151).","No maximum purchase price, but the loan must fit income and ratios; no cash-out refinance (streamlined-assist refinance available).","Seller concessions limited to 6 percent; July 2026 proposed rule would exclude real estate commissions from the cap.","Minimum credit score 640 for GUS accept streamlined documentation; lower scores manually underwritten.","Property must be modest, no income-producing structures, no in-ground pools restriction removed in 2021 (verify).","Occupancy within 60 days; borrower cannot own another adequate dwelling (with exceptions)."],broker:"Steven should check the USDA map for Temecula/Menifee/Winchester, Fallbrook/Valley Center, and Nellis-area addresses because 100 percent financing with a lower annual fee than FHA often wins for moderate-income buyers. He must count all adult household income against the 2026 limit at pre-qualification and confirm the lender's GUS findings. He should watch the July 2026 fee change and the commission-exclusion proposal when quoting seller-paid costs.",penalties:"Lender indemnification and loss of approval; False Claims Act exposure for false certifications.",eff:"Section 502 guaranteed program 1991; 7 CFR 3555 effective Dec 1, 2014; 2026 income limits effective July 13, 2026.",src:[{t:"7 CFR 3555 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-7/subtitle-B/chapter-XXXV/part-3555"},{t:"USDA 2026 income limits (Pennymac summary of PN 657)",u:"https://corr.pennymac.com/announcements/announcement-26-82"},{t:"USDA proposed rule: commissions excluded from 6 percent (Federal Register 2026-07617 summary)",u:"https://policyrisk.com/federal-register/2026-07617"}],verify:true},
{k:"appraisal_independence",n:"Appraisal Independence Requirements (AIR) and Reg Z 1026.42",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1639e; 12 CFR 1026.42; Fannie Mae/Freddie Mac AIR; 12 CFR Part 34 Subpart C (OCC)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB (Reg Z) / FHFA-GSEs (AIR) / HUD",topic:["appraisal","origination"],summary:"Appraisal independence rules prohibit anyone with an interest in the transaction from coercing, bribing or pressuring an appraiser, from selecting an appraiser based on expected value, and from paying less than customary and reasonable fees. Reg Z 1026.42 applies to all consumer credit secured by a principal dwelling; the GSE Appraiser Independence Requirements apply to loans sold to Fannie and Freddie and bar loan production staff (including brokers) from ordering or communicating substantively with appraisers. Brokers may still ask for a reconsideration of value through the lender's process, which the 2024 interagency ROV guidance formalized.",applies:"Creditors, mortgage brokers, loan officers, real estate agents, AMCs and appraisers on dwelling-secured consumer loans; AIR to all GSE deliveries.",key:["No coercion, extortion, inducement, bribery or intimidation of an appraiser to hit a value (1026.42(c)(1)).","No conditioning compensation on a value, no withholding payment because of a value, no providing a target value except a copy of the purchase contract (1026.42(c)).","Person preparing the valuation must not have a financial interest in the transaction (1026.42(d)).","Mandatory reporting of appraiser misconduct to the state licensing agency within a reasonable time (1026.42(g)).","Customary and reasonable fees must be paid to fee appraisers (1026.42(f)).","GSE AIR: loan production staff (anyone compensated on a commission basis tied to closings) may not select, retain, recommend or communicate about value with the appraiser; brokers may not order appraisals for GSE loans (AIR Section III-IV).","Appraisal transfers between lenders are allowed if AIR was followed; a second appraisal may not be ordered to obtain a higher value unless under a documented QC policy.","Appraiser may communicate with the borrower, agent or broker for factual information (access, comparables) but not about value.","HPML appraisal rule: full interior appraisal by a licensed appraiser, plus a second appraisal on flips (10 percent within 90 days, 20 percent within 180 days) (1026.35(c)).","ROV: lenders must have a documented reconsideration-of-value process for borrowers; brokers submit through the lender (interagency guidance July 2024; FHA ML 2024-07; Fannie/Freddie Oct 31, 2024)."],broker:"Steven must not call an appraiser to discuss value, send a desired value, or threaten to stop using them. For GSE loans his lender or its AMC orders the appraisal; for some non-QM lenders he may be allowed to order through an AMC but still cannot steer. When a value comes in low he should assemble a factual ROV package (missed comparables, errors) and submit it through the lender's ROV process rather than shopping lenders for a second appraisal. He should report an appraiser only for genuine USPAP or licensing violations, through the lender.",penalties:"TILA civil penalties: the greater of actual damages or $10,000 per day for the first violation and $20,000 per day for subsequent (15 U.S.C. 1639e(k)); loan unsaleable to GSEs; license discipline; appraiser complaints.",eff:"HVCC May 2009; Dodd-Frank 1472 and Reg Z 1026.42 effective April 1, 2011; AIR updated 2010 and 2024 (ROV); interagency ROV guidance July 2024.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1026.42 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026/subpart-E/section-1026.42"},{t:"Fannie Mae Appraiser Independence Requirements",u:"https://singlefamily.fanniemae.com/originating-underwriting/appraiser-independence-requirements"},{t:"Interagency ROV guidance summary (HLC)",u:"https://www.hlc.com/en/publications/final-interagency-guidance-on-reconsiderations-of-value-of-residential-real-estate"}],verify:false},
{k:"ecoa_valuations",n:"ECOA Valuations Rule (copies of appraisals and valuations)",cite:"12 CFR 1002.14; 15 U.S.C. 1691(e)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["appraisal","origination"],summary:"The ECOA valuations rule requires creditors to give applicants free copies of every written valuation developed in connection with an application for a first-lien loan on a dwelling, promptly after completion or 3 business days before consummation, whichever is earlier. Applicants must be told of this right within 3 business days of application. The rule covers appraisals, AVMs, desk reviews and broker price opinions used by the creditor. The applicant may waive the 3-day timing but still must receive copies at or before closing. It replaced the old FCRA appraisal-copy rule and the Reg B right to copies on request.",applies:"Creditors on applications for credit to be secured by a first lien on a dwelling, whether consumer or business purpose; brokers must help ensure delivery.",key:["Notice of the right to receive copies of valuations within 3 business days of receiving the application (1002.14(a)(2)).","Provide copies of all written valuations promptly upon completion or 3 business days before consummation (closed-end) or account opening (open-end), whichever is earlier (1002.14(a)(1)).","Copies must be provided even if the loan is denied, withdrawn or incomplete (within 30 days of determination).","Applicant may waive the 3-day timing at least 3 business days before consummation, but must still receive copies at consummation (1002.14(a)(1)).","Creditor may charge a reasonable fee for the appraisal but not for the copy (1002.14(a)(3)).","Valuation includes any estimate of value developed in connection with the application: appraisals, AVMs, BPOs, desk reviews, CU/LCA findings used in the decision (1002.14(b)(3)).","Electronic delivery with E-SIGN consent allowed.","Business-purpose first liens (DSCR investor loans) are covered because the rule applies regardless of purpose.","Reg B record retention 25 months.","Works with Reg Z HPML appraisal rule and the GSE ROV process."],broker:"Steven should confirm the lender's initial disclosure package includes the valuations notice and should push the appraisal (and any AVM or desk review) to the borrower the day he receives it, since the 3-business-day pre-closing deadline is easy to miss on fast closings. If a borrower wants to close faster he can collect a timing waiver at least 3 business days before closing. On DSCR loans he should still deliver the appraisal to the investor-borrower.",penalties:"ECOA remedies: actual and punitive damages up to $10,000 (individual), attorney fees; CFPB penalties; frequent exam finding.",eff:"Effective Jan 18, 2014.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1002.14 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1002/section-1002.14"},{t:"CFPB ECOA valuations rule small entity guide",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/ecoa-valuations-rule/"}],verify:false},
{k:"fannie_freddie",n:"Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac Selling Guides (conventional conforming)",cite:"Fannie Mae Single Family Selling Guide; Freddie Mac Single-Family Seller/Servicer Guide; 12 U.S.C. 1716 et seq., 1451 et seq.",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FHFA (conservator/regulator) / Fannie Mae / Freddie Mac",topic:["origination","appraisal","closing"],summary:"The GSEs buy most conventional loans in the U.S. and their Selling Guides, DU and LPA findings effectively define conforming underwriting: credit, income, assets, LTV, property, condo and appraisal rules, plus pricing through loan-level price adjustments. Brokers follow the Guides through their wholesale lenders. Since 2023 the GSEs allow 5 percent down on owner-occupied 2-4 units, expanded appraisal waivers (value acceptance) in DU 12.0 (Jan 2025), opened VantageScore 4.0 to all sellers (Sept 2026), and are moving to UAD 3.6 / the new URAR (mandatory Nov 2, 2026 with a one-time exception window to May 19, 2027). Both remain in conservatorship; Treasury said in October 2026 that an exit is not imminent.",applies:"Loans sold to Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac by approved seller/servicers; brokers' loans must meet the Guide plus lender overlays.",key:["2026 conforming limits: $832,750 baseline one-unit; high-cost ceiling $1,249,125; 2-4 unit limits higher (FHFA Nov 25, 2025).","Minimum down payment 3 percent (HomeReady/Home Possible/Conventional 97 for first-time buyers or income-limited); 5 percent on owner-occupied 2-4 units (Fannie since Nov 18, 2023; Freddie similar); 15 percent second home; 15-25 percent investment.","Minimum credit score 620 (DU); representative score rules; VantageScore 4.0 accepted from all sellers effective Sept 9, 2026 (LL-2026-06 / Bulletin 2026-H); tri-merge still required, bi-merge under consideration (verify).","Maximum DTI 50 percent with DU Approve/Eligible; 45 percent manual (36 percent without reserves).","LLPA matrix (updated Sept 30, 2026) prices by credit score and LTV; LLPA waivers for first-time buyers at or below 100 percent AMI (120 percent in high-cost) and Duty to Serve loans; waiver determination date moves to DU casefile create date Jan 15, 2027.","Appraisal: value acceptance (waiver), value acceptance + property data, hybrid, or traditional appraisal based on DU/LPA; UAD 3.6/new URAR mandatory for UCDP submissions Nov 2, 2026 (exception to May 19, 2027).","Condo project review (full, limited, PERS); ineligible projects list; FL condo structural/reserve requirements after Surfside (Lender Letter LL-2021-14 and later).","Income: 2 years self-employment (1 year with 5 years prior), rental income with Form 1007/1025 (75 percent), boarder income for HomeReady, asset depletion.","Seller contributions: 3 percent at LTV over 90, 6 percent at 75.01-90, 9 percent at 75 or below; investment 2 percent.","Temporary buydowns, ARMs (SOFR), 40-year terms not eligible; RefiNow/Refi Possible for low-income borrowers.","Area Median Income limits updated annually (2026 limits effective June 13, 2026)."],broker:"Steven should run DU or LPA early to capture value acceptance offers and LLPA waivers (many Temecula and Las Vegas first-time buyers are under the AMI cap), and quote LLPAs accurately when comparing conventional to FHA. He should prepare for the UAD 3.6 report format (narrative changes, new fields) on everything submitted from Nov 2, 2026 and ask each lender whether it took the exception. He should confirm whether his credit vendor can deliver VantageScore 4.0 if a lender wants it, and keep watching FHFA on bi-merge. Condo files in Florida need early project review because of the structural reserve rules.",penalties:"Repurchase and make-whole demands against the lender, which flow down to broker agreements (indemnification), loss of lender relationships, and fraud referrals to FHFA-OIG.",eff:"Fannie Mae 1938/1968; Freddie Mac 1970; conservatorship Sept 6, 2008; DU 12.0 Jan 2025; VantageScore open Sept 2026; UAD 3.6 mandate Nov 2, 2026.",src:[{t:"Fannie Mae Selling Guide",u:"https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/"},{t:"Freddie Mac Seller/Servicer Guide",u:"https://guide.freddiemac.com/"},{t:"Fannie Mae UAD announcements",u:"https://singlefamily.fanniemae.com/delivering/uniform-mortgage-data-program/uniform-appraisal-dataset/uniform-appraisal-dataset-announcements"},{t:"Fannie Mae LLPA matrix (Sept 30, 2026)",u:"https://singlefamily.fanniemae.com/media/9391/display"}],verify:true},
{k:"ginnie",n:"Ginnie Mae MBS Program (government loan securitization)",cite:"12 U.S.C. 1716b, 1721; 24 CFR Part 300 et seq.; Ginnie Mae MBS Guide 5500.3",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"Government National Mortgage Association (HUD)",topic:["origination","servicing"],summary:"Ginnie Mae guarantees timely payment on securities backed by FHA, VA, USDA and HUD-184 loans, which is what makes government lending liquid. Issuers must meet net worth, liquidity and capital standards and follow pooling rules, including seasoning limits on refinances that were written after the VA churning scandal. Brokers do not deal with Ginnie Mae directly, but its rules drive lender overlays on government loans: the 210-day/6-payment seasoning for streamline and IRRRL refinances, restrictions on cash-out refinance pooling, and net tangible benefit requirements. Ginnie Mae's financial requirements also explain why some wholesale lenders restrict government products.",applies:"Approved issuers of Ginnie Mae MBS and the loans they pool; indirectly every FHA/VA/USDA loan intended for sale.",key:["Issuer net worth minimum $2.5 million plus 0.35 percent of outstanding single-family obligations; liquidity 0.10 percent; risk-based capital 6 percent (MBS Guide Ch. 2-3; 2024 RBC requirement).","Refinance seasoning: loan must have 6 monthly payments made and 210 days from first payment due date before an IRRRL, FHA streamline or any refinance is pool-eligible (APM 19-05, APM 18-04).","VA cash-out refinances over 90 percent LTV are ineligible for standard pools (APM 19-05).","Loans must be insured or guaranteed by FHA, VA, USDA or HUD Section 184; pooled at par with servicing fees of 19-69 bps (Ginnie II).","Issuers advance principal and interest to investors when borrowers do not pay, which is why government servicing is capital-intensive.","Ginnie Mae digital collateral (eNotes) program accepts electronic promissory notes from approved issuers.","Issuers must report monthly and are subject to default and buyout rules (loans 90+ days delinquent may be bought out of pools).","Term sheets limit pooling of loans with interest rates far from the pass-through rate.","HUD-184 Native American loans and Title I manufactured home loans have their own pools."],broker:"Steven should understand that his government-loan rate sheets and the 210-day/6-payment seasoning rules on streamlines and IRRRLs come from Ginnie Mae pooling rules, not from FHA or VA alone, and he should not promise a streamline refinance before the seasoning date. When a wholesale lender tightens government overlays it is often a Ginnie Mae capital issue, so having several government-strong lenders on his panel protects his military and FHA pipeline.",penalties:"Issuer sanctions (default, termination, loss of servicing); no direct broker penalties, but loans that cannot be pooled are repriced or repurchased.",eff:"Created 1968; Ginnie II program 1983; refinance seasoning APMs 2018-2019; risk-based capital effective Dec 31, 2024.",src:[{t:"Ginnie Mae MBS Guide",u:"https://www.ginniemae.gov/issuers/program_guidelines/Pages/mbs_guide.aspx"},{t:"Ginnie Mae APM 19-05",u:"https://www.ginniemae.gov/issuers/program_guidelines/Pages/mbsguideapmslibdisppage.aspx?ParamID=94"}],verify:false},
{k:"fhfa_limits",n:"FHFA Conforming Loan Limits (2026)",cite:"12 U.S.C. 1717(b)(2), 1454(a)(2); HERA Section 1124",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"Federal Housing Finance Agency",topic:["origination"],summary:"Each November FHFA sets the maximum loan size Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac may buy for the next year, based on the change in the FHFA House Price Index. For 2026 the baseline one-unit limit is $832,750 (up 3.26 percent from $806,500), and the ceiling for high-cost areas is $1,249,125 (150 percent of baseline); Alaska, Hawaii, Guam and the USVI use $1,249,125 as the floor and $1,873,675 as the ceiling. County-level limits between floor and ceiling are set at 115 percent of the local median home price. FHA floors and ceilings (65 percent and 150 percent of baseline) and VA full-entitlement calculations key off these numbers.",applies:"Loans delivered to Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac with note dates on or after Jan 1, 2026; FHA, VA and many jumbo investors reference the same figures.",key:["2026 baseline: $832,750 (1 unit), $1,066,250 (2 units), $1,288,750 (3 units), $1,601,750 (4 units) (verify multi-unit figures).","2026 ceiling (high-cost): $1,249,125 (1 unit), up to $2,402,625 for 4 units (verify).","2025 baseline was $806,500 and ceiling $1,209,750.","High-cost county limits = 115 percent of median home price, capped at the ceiling; Los Angeles and Orange counties are at the ceiling; San Diego County 2026 one-unit limit about $1,209,750-$1,249,125 (verify exact); Riverside County near baseline (verify); Clark County, Maricopa County, Cook County and Miami-Dade at or near baseline (verify).","Limits apply by note date, not application date; many lenders accept new limits on locks in December ahead of Jan 1.","High-balance (super-conforming) loans between baseline and county limit carry extra LLPAs.","FHA 2026 floor $541,287 and ceiling $1,249,125 derive from these limits (65 percent / 150 percent).","VA: with full entitlement there is no cap; with partial entitlement VA guarantees 25 percent of the county conforming limit.","Limits are published at fhfa.gov with a county lookup file each late November."],broker:"Steven should update his quoting tools each December with the county file (San Diego, Riverside, Orange, LA, Clark, Maricopa, Cook, Miami-Dade at minimum) and know which of his lenders honor new limits early. Loans just above a county limit can often be restructured with a slightly larger down payment or a piggyback to stay conforming and avoid jumbo pricing. He should note the countylimits.js data file in this dashboard may need a 2026 refresh.",penalties:"None directly; loans above limits are ineligible for GSE delivery.",eff:"2026 limits announced Nov 25, 2025, effective Jan 1, 2026; 2027 limits expected late Nov 2026.",src:[{t:"FHFA conforming loan limits page",u:"https://www.fhfa.gov/data/conforming-loan-limit-cll-values"},{t:"Orrick: FHFA announces 2026 limits",u:"https://infobytes.orrick.com/2025-12-05/fhfa-announces-2026-increases-to-conforming-loan-limits/"},{t:"NAHB: 2026 conforming limits",u:"https://www.nahb.org/blog/2025/11/2026-conforming-loan-limits"}],verify:true},
{k:"fdcpa",n:"Fair Debt Collection Practices Act / Regulation F",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1692 et seq.; 12 CFR Part 1006",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["servicing"],summary:"The FDCPA regulates third-party debt collectors collecting consumer debts: when and how they may contact consumers, what they must disclose, and what they may not say or do. Mortgage servicers are debt collectors only if they acquire a loan already in default, and originators are generally not covered. Regulation F (2021) added call-frequency limits, validation notice content, and rules for emails and texts. Brokers encounter the FDCPA when helping borrowers resolve collections on their credit report, when a servicer that bought a defaulted loan communicates with a client, and through state analogs like California's Rosenthal Act, which covers original creditors too.",applies:"Third-party collectors and debt buyers of consumer debts; mortgage servicers that obtained servicing after default; attorneys who regularly collect; not original creditors (federally) and not brokers collecting their own fees.",key:["Validation notice within 5 days of initial communication with itemization, dispute rights and the 30-day validation period (1006.34).","Call frequency presumption: no more than 7 calls in 7 days per debt, and no call within 7 days of a conversation (1006.14(b)).","Contact hours 8 a.m. to 9 p.m. local; no contact at work if the employer prohibits; no contact if represented by an attorney (1006.6).","Email and text allowed with opt-out instructions; limited-content voicemail message safe harbor (1006.2(j), 1006.6(d)).","No false, deceptive or misleading representations (1006.18) and no harassment or abuse (1006.14).","No collection of amounts not authorized by the agreement or law (1006.22(b)).","Time-barred debt: no suits or threats of suit (1006.26).","Credit reporting only after communicating with the consumer first (1006.30(a)).","Cease-communication requests must be honored (1006.6(c)).","Mortgage servicers subject to Reg F must coordinate with Reg X early intervention and loss mitigation rules (1006.6(b)(4), 1024.39)."],broker:"Steven does not act as a debt collector, but when a client's credit report shows collections he should coach them to request validation in writing and dispute inaccurate items (supporting a rapid rescore) rather than paying unverified debts. If a client's servicer is a debt buyer, the client has FDCPA and Rosenthal rights that can help in a loss-mitigation dispute. He must not himself make collection-style calls about unpaid broker fees that violate the Rosenthal Act in California.",penalties:"Actual damages, statutory damages up to $1,000 per action (class actions lesser of $500,000 or 1 percent of net worth), attorney fees; CFPB and FTC enforcement; 1-year statute of limitations.",eff:"Enacted 1977; Regulation F effective Nov 30, 2021.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1006 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1006"},{t:"CFPB debt collection rule resources",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/debt-collection-resources/debt-collection-rule/"}],verify:false},
{k:"cfpb_servicing_rules",n:"CFPB Mortgage Servicing Rules (Regulation X Subpart C and Regulation Z servicing provisions)",cite:"12 CFR 1024.30-1024.41; 12 CFR 1026.20(c)-(e), 1026.36(c), 1026.39, 1026.41",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["servicing"],summary:"The 2013-2014 servicing rules set national standards for how mortgage servicers handle payments, escrow, errors, information requests, force-placed insurance, early intervention with delinquent borrowers, continuity of contact and loss mitigation. The core protections are a ban on dual tracking (no foreclosure filing until 120 days delinquent and no sale while a complete loss mitigation application is pending), a single point of contact, and strict response timelines. The CFPB's July 2026 agenda lists a servicing 'streamlining' rule and rescission of COVID-era provisions, so changes are likely in late 2026, but current rules apply. Brokers meet these rules when advising clients in default or when the client's rate-term refinance depends on a servicer's payoff or escrow accounting.",applies:"Servicers of federally related mortgage loans; small servicers (5,000 or fewer loans serviced and owned/originated) are exempt from several provisions; brokers are not servicers.",key:["Payments must be credited the day received; partial payments may be held in suspense with periodic statement disclosure (1026.36(c)).","Periodic statements each billing cycle with specified content; coupon books allowed for fixed-rate loans (1026.41).","Error resolution: acknowledge notices of error within 5 business days, resolve within 30 business days (45 with extension) (1024.35).","Information requests: same timelines; payoff requests within 7 business days (1024.36; 1026.36(c)(3)).","Force-placed insurance: two notices (45 days and 15 days) before charging; refund if borrower had coverage (1024.37).","Early intervention: live contact by day 36 of delinquency, written notice by day 45 (1024.39).","Continuity of contact: assign personnel by day 45 (1024.40).","Loss mitigation: acknowledge application within 5 business days, evaluate complete applications within 30 days, appeal rights; no foreclosure referral until 120 days delinquent; no foreclosure sale while a complete application is pending (1024.41).","Successors in interest (heirs, divorce) treated as borrowers after confirmation (1024.30(d), 1024.38(b)(1)(vi)).","Escrow: annual analysis, shortage/surplus rules, timely payment of taxes and insurance (1024.17, 1024.34).","ARM reset notices (1026.20(c)-(d)); transfer notices (1024.33)."],broker:"When a client misses payments Steven should explain the 120-day pre-foreclosure window, the right to a single point of contact and the loss-mitigation application process, and encourage a complete application quickly because that is what stops a sale. For refinances he should order payoffs early (7-business-day rule) and watch escrow shortages that change the new loan's impound setup. When a servicer mishandles an escrow or a payment he can help the client send a written notice of error, which forces a 30-day investigation. He should also stay current on the late-2026 servicing rule changes and state overlays (CA HBOR, NV HOBR).",penalties:"RESPA Section 6 damages (actual plus up to $2,000 statutory for pattern or practice), TILA remedies for Reg Z servicing provisions, CFPB and state enforcement; foreclosure challenges under state HBORs.",eff:"Effective Jan 10, 2014; amended Oct 19, 2017 and April 19, 2018 (successors, periodic statements in bankruptcy); COVID amendments 2020-2021; CFPB proposed streamlining July 2024 (not final); 2026 agenda targets Aug-Nov 2026.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1024 Subpart C (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1024/subpart-C"},{t:"CFPB mortgage servicing resources",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/mortgage-servicing/"},{t:"CFPB 2026 regulatory agenda (Troutman)",u:"https://www.consumerfinancialserviceslawmonitor.com/2026/07/cfpb-releases-2026-regulatory-agenda-detailing-planned-rulemakings/"}],verify:false},
{k:"hmda_reporting_thresholds",n:"HMDA Reporting Thresholds and 2026 Asset Exemption",cite:"12 CFR 1003.2(g), 1003.3(c); 90 FR (Jan 2026 asset adjustment)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["fair-lending"],summary:"Whether a lender must report HMDA data depends on three tests: institution type and asset size (for depositories), location (an office in an MSA), and loan volume. The volume test is 25 closed-end mortgages or 200 open-end lines in each of the two preceding years. The CFPB's attempt to raise the closed-end threshold to 100 was vacated in September 2022, and the Bureau confirmed 25 loans applies. For 2026 the depository asset exemption rose to $59 million. A March 2026 executive order asked the CFPB to consider raising thresholds and narrowing scope, so a proposal may appear, but nothing has changed yet.",applies:"Nonbank lenders and depositories that originate loans secured by dwellings; brokers who do not fund in their own name are not reporters.",key:["Closed-end threshold: 25 loans in each of the two preceding calendar years (1003.2(g)(1)(v)(A) as modified by the 2022 vacatur).","Open-end threshold: 200 lines in each of the two preceding years (1003.2(g)(1)(v)(B)).","Depository asset exemption: $59 million as of Dec 31, 2025 for 2026 data (adjusted Jan 2026).","Location test: a home or branch office in an MSA, or (for nonbanks) a defined volume of loans in an MSA.","Nonbank test: originated at least 25 closed-end (or 200 open-end) in each of the prior 2 years AND is for-profit AND meets the location test (1003.2(g)(2)).","Partial exemption for insured depositories and credit unions under 500 closed-end or 500 open-end loans (EGRRCPA Section 104).","CFPB stated it would not cite violations for 2020-2022 data from institutions with 25-99 loans after the vacatur.","Executive order (March 13, 2026, 'Promoting Access to Mortgage Credit') directs CFPB to consider raising asset exemptions and excluding inquiries from HMDA scope (verify title/date).","Data submitted via the HMDA Platform by March 1; LAR formatting per the Filing Instructions Guide."],broker:"Steven should recount originations each January if Patriot Pacific ever funds in its own name; at 25 closed-end loans in two consecutive years it becomes a HMDA reporter with full data collection. As a pure broker he only needs to collect demographic information correctly for his lenders. He should monitor CFPB proposals in late 2026 for threshold increases.",penalties:"Civil money penalties; resubmission; fair-lending referrals.",eff:"25-loan closed-end threshold restored Sept 23, 2022 (court order) and confirmed Dec 6, 2022; 2026 asset exemption effective Jan 7, 2026.",src:[{t:"CFPB blog: changes to HMDA closed-end threshold",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/archive/blog/changes-to-hmda-closed-end-loan-reporting-threshold/"},{t:"CFPB 2026 HMDA asset exemption (Ballard)",u:"https://www.consumerfinancemonitor.com/2026/01/07/cfpb-adjusts-hmda-asset-exemption-threshold-2/"}],verify:true},
{k:"red_flags_rule",n:"Identity Theft Red Flags Rule and Address Discrepancy Rule",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1681m(e); 16 CFR Part 681; 12 CFR 1022.82, 1022.90-1022.91",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FTC / CFPB",topic:["data-security","privacy","origination"],summary:"Creditors that maintain covered accounts, including mortgage brokers and lenders, must have a written identity theft prevention program that identifies red flags, detects them, responds appropriately and is updated periodically. A related rule requires users of consumer reports to have procedures for handling a notice of address discrepancy from a credit bureau. The program must be approved by the board or senior management and staff must be trained. Mortgage fraud involving stolen identities and synthetic identities makes this a practical, not theoretical, requirement for brokers.",applies:"Financial institutions and creditors with covered accounts (consumer accounts involving multiple payments or any account with a reasonably foreseeable risk of identity theft); mortgage brokers qualify as creditors who regularly arrange credit.",key:["Written identity theft prevention program approved by board or senior management (16 CFR 681.1(e)).","Identify relevant red flags from the five categories: alerts from bureaus, suspicious documents, suspicious personal identifying information, unusual account activity, notices from victims or law enforcement (681.1 App. A).","Detect red flags at account opening (verify identity) and during the relationship (681.1(d)(2)(ii)).","Respond appropriately: additional verification, declining the application, notifying the consumer, filing a SAR where required (681.1(d)(2)(iii)).","Update the program periodically for new fraud patterns (681.1(d)(2)(iv)).","Train staff and oversee service providers (681.1(e)(3)-(4)).","Address discrepancy: when a bureau sends a notice of address discrepancy, form a reasonable belief the report relates to the consumer and furnish a confirmed address back to the bureau if a relationship is established (12 CFR 1022.82).","Fraud alerts and active-duty alerts on a credit file require contact with the consumer before extending credit (15 U.S.C. 1681c-1(h)).","Credit freezes must be lifted by the consumer; the broker cannot pull a frozen file."],broker:"Steven should keep a short written red flags program (identity verification steps at application, document authenticity checks, handling of fraud alerts and address mismatches), train any staff annually, and integrate it with his AML program and FTC Safeguards program. On every file he should compare the ID, SSN, address history and employer against the credit report and the 4506-C/IRS transcript, and treat an active-duty alert as a requirement to call the servicemember at the number on the alert.",penalties:"FTC and CFPB enforcement; civil penalties for knowing violations; FCRA private liability for address-discrepancy failures; losses from fraud loans and repurchases.",eff:"Effective Nov 1, 2008 (enforced from Jan 1, 2011 after the Red Flag Program Clarification Act of 2010).",src:[{t:"16 CFR 681 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-681"},{t:"FTC Red Flags Rule guide",u:"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/fighting-identity-theft-red-flags-rule-how-guide-business"}],verify:false},
{k:"fcra_trigger_leads",n:"Mortgage Trigger Leads and the Homebuyers Privacy Protection Act of 2025",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1681b(c) as amended by Pub. L. 119-37 (H.R. 2808) (verify public law number)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / FTC",topic:["privacy","telemarketing","advertising"],summary:"A trigger lead is a prescreened list sold by a credit bureau when a consumer's credit is pulled for a mortgage, letting competitors bombard the applicant with calls and texts within hours. The Homebuyers Privacy Protection Act, signed September 5, 2025 and effective March 5, 2026, amended FCRA so bureaus may furnish a mortgage-inquiry trigger lead only if the recipient has a firm offer of credit and either the consumer authorized the solicitation or the recipient originated the consumer's current mortgage, services it, or is a bank or credit union holding the consumer's account. For brokers this both ends most competitor poaching and cuts off a lead source; buying trigger leads from non-compliant vendors now creates FCRA liability.",applies:"Consumer reporting agencies furnishing prescreened lists tied to mortgage inquiries; lenders, brokers and lead sellers who buy or use them; consumers' opt-out rights continue.",key:["Prohibition: a CRA may not furnish a consumer report in connection with a credit transaction that is a residential mortgage inquiry unless the exception applies (new 1681b(c)(1)(B) language, verify).","Exception 1: the consumer authorized the CRA to furnish the report for the specific solicitation.","Exception 2: the recipient has a firm offer of credit AND (a) originated the consumer's current residential mortgage, (b) services it, or (c) is an insured depository or credit union with a current account for the consumer.","Trigger leads sold before March 5, 2026 remain lawful to use (legacy data).","Consumers may still opt out of all prescreened offers at 888-5-OPTOUT (OptOutPrescreen.com) for 5 years or permanently in writing.","Non-mortgage prescreened offers are unaffected.","Lead vendors offering 'mortgage inquiry' or 'credit-triggered' data after March 2026 must document the exception or are selling unlawfully; buyers can face FCRA user liability.","State mini-trigger-lead laws (e.g., limits in several states) remain in force where stricter.","Firm offer of credit still requires the Reg V prescreen opt-out notice format when used."],broker:"Steven should stop buying any lead product described as trigger, inquiry-triggered or credit-event mortgage data unless the vendor proves one of the exceptions, and should keep the vendor's written representation. He can reassure borrowers at application that competitor calls should drop sharply after a pull, and still recommend OptOutPrescreen and the Do Not Call registry. On the retention side, he and his servicing partners are allowed to receive notices on their own past customers under the originator/servicer exception, which is useful for refinance timing.",penalties:"FCRA civil liability for bureaus and users (negligent: actual damages; willful: $100-$1,000 statutory per consumer plus punitive), FTC/CFPB enforcement.",eff:"Signed Sept 5, 2025; effective March 5, 2026 (some sources say March 4; verify).",src:[{t:"NAMB: Understanding the Trigger Leads Ban",u:"https://www.namb.org/understanding-the-trigger-leads-ban"},{t:"NAR: trigger leads law takes effect",u:"https://www.nar.realtor/news/real-estate-news/protecting-home-buyers-privacy-congress-moves-to-curb-abusive-trigger-leads"},{t:"National Mortgage Professional: restrictions begin",u:"https://nationalmortgageprofessional.com/news/trigger-lead-restrictions-begin-homebuyers-privacy-protection-act-takes-effect"}],verify:true},
{k:"avm_qc_rule",n:"Automated Valuation Model Quality Control Standards (interagency rule, 2024) and appraisal-bias controls",cite:"15 U.S.C. 1639e(i); 12 CFR 1026.42(h) (CFPB); 12 CFR 34 Subpart F (OCC); 12 CFR 1222 (FHFA) and parallel parts",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB, OCC, Fed, FDIC, NCUA, FHFA",topic:["appraisal","fair-lending","origination"],summary:"Dodd-Frank Section 1473(q) required regulators to set quality control standards for automated valuation models used in mortgage origination and securitization. The final rule, published August 7, 2024 and effective October 1, 2025, requires mortgage originators and secondary market issuers that use AVMs to make credit decisions or covered securitization determinations to adopt policies that ensure confidence in estimates, protect against data manipulation, avoid conflicts of interest, require random sample testing, and comply with nondiscrimination laws. It is paired with the 2024 ROV guidance and the GSEs' bias-related appraisal policies (prohibited language in reports, ROV procedures). The rule is principles-based and applies to lenders rather than brokers, but brokers must understand which valuation product is used and how to challenge it.",applies:"Mortgage originators (creditors) and secondary market issuers using AVMs to determine collateral value for a credit decision, including value acceptance programs; AVMs used only for monitoring, marketing or QC review are out of scope.",key:["Five factors: random sample testing and reviews; avoidance of data manipulation; avoidance of conflicts of interest; confidence in estimates; compliance with applicable nondiscrimination laws (1026.42(h)(1)).","Flexible, risk-based policies and procedures; no prescribed metrics.","Effective date Oct 1, 2025 (first day of the quarter 12 months after publication).","Covers AVMs used in value-acceptance decisions and in determining whether to waive an appraisal, if the originator relies on the AVM for the credit decision (preamble).","Does not cover appraisers using AVMs as a tool in an appraisal.","ROV guidance (July 2024): lenders should give borrowers a clear process to request reconsideration, accept information about errors and omitted comparables, and respond in writing; FHA ML 2024-07 (effective Oct 31, 2024 after extension) and GSE Selling Guide updates make ROV mandatory for those loans.","GSE appraisal reports may not use prohibited words or references to protected classes (Fannie Mae Appraiser Update and Selling Guide B4-1.1-04).","Appraisal subcommittee and PAVE task force initiatives feed into exam expectations on appraisal bias.","CA AB 948 (2021) requires a fair-appraisal notice in every CA sale contract and appraisal, and DRE/BREA complaint routes."],broker:"Steven should know, for each lender he uses, whether a given file is valued by an AVM/value acceptance, a hybrid, or a traditional appraisal, since the ROV path and the borrower's remedies differ. When a borrower believes a valuation is low or biased he should prepare a factual ROV with specific comparables and submit it through the lender, and in California give the AB 948 fair appraisal notice and the BREA complaint information. He should not try to shop AVM results across lenders to pick the highest.",penalties:"Agency enforcement against lenders (TILA 1639e civil penalties), fair-lending referrals; repurchase of loans where value acceptance was misused.",eff:"Final rule published Aug 7, 2024; effective Oct 1, 2025; ROV guidance final July 2024 (FR July 26, 2024).",src:[{t:"CFPB press release: AVM final rule",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/about-us/newsroom/agencies-issue-final-rule-to-help-ensure-credibility-and-integrity-of-automated-valuation-models"},{t:"Mayer Brown: AVM rule now effective (Oct 2025)",u:"https://www.mayerbrown.com/en/insights/publications/2025/10/mortgage-originators-and-secondary-market-participants-take-note-the-final-rule-on-automated-valuation-models-is-now-effective-and-could-apply-to-you"},{t:"CFPB AVM small entity compliance guide",u:"https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/documents/cfpb_quality-control-standards-for-avm-small-entity-compliance-guide_2024-10.pdf"}],verify:false},
{k:"reg_z_qm_price_based",n:"General QM Price-Based Definition and 2026 Thresholds",cite:"12 CFR 1026.43(e)(2); 85 FR 86308 (Dec 29, 2020)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB",topic:["origination"],summary:"Since October 1, 2022 the General QM is defined by the loan's APR spread over APOR instead of a 43 percent DTI and Appendix Q. A first-lien loan is a General QM if the APR does not exceed APOR by more than 2.25 percentage points (higher spreads allowed for smaller loans and subordinate liens), the loan has no risky features, the term is 30 years or less, points and fees are within the cap, and the creditor considers and verifies income, assets and debts. Safe harbor applies under APOR plus 1.5; between 1.5 and 2.25 the loan is a rebuttable-presumption QM. The dollar thresholds for points and fees and the small-loan spread tiers index every January.",applies:"Creditors on closed-end consumer dwelling-secured loans seeking QM status; brokers price to stay within the spread and fee caps.",key:["Spread thresholds (first lien): 2.25 points for loans of about $130,461 or more (2025); 3.5 points for about $78,277-$130,460; 6.5 points for about $26,092-$78,276; 6.5 points for about $16,308-$26,091; 6.5 points below (loan amount tiers index annually; 2026 figures verify).","Subordinate liens: 3.5 points for loans of about $78,277 or more; 6.5 points below.","Safe harbor at APR less than APOR + 1.5 (first lien) / + 3.5 (subordinate) (1026.43(e)(1)(i)); rebuttable presumption above.","ARMs with initial fixed period under 5 years: APR computed using the maximum rate in the first 5 years (1026.43(e)(2)(vi)).","Points and fees cap: 3 percent for loans at or above about $130,461 (2025); $3,913 for $78,277-$130,460; 5 percent for $26,092-$78,276; $1,304 for $16,308-$26,091; 8 percent below $16,308 (2026 amounts adjust; verify).","Consider and verify using reasonably reliable third-party records; the rule lists safe-harbor verification standards (Fannie/Freddie Guides, FHA, VA, USDA handbooks as of specified dates) (1026.43(e)(2)(v)(B)).","No negative amortization, interest-only, balloon; term 30 years or less; regular periodic payments.","Temporary GSE QM (the patch) expired for applications on or after Oct 1, 2022 (practically July 1, 2021 for GSE delivery).","Seasoned QM alternative for portfolio loans; small creditor QMs unchanged.","CFPB 2026 agenda flags ATR/QM for pre-rule activity in August 2026; no proposal published as of Oct 2026 (verify)."],broker:"On every conventional and non-QM quote Steven should check the APR-to-APOR spread (APOR tables publish weekly on the FFIEC site) to know whether the loan is safe-harbor QM, rebuttable QM or non-QM, because it changes pricing and lender eligibility. Lender-paid broker compensation counts toward points and fees, so on small loans (common in Chicago and Phoenix exurbs) he must watch the 3 percent and tiered caps. He should keep the 2026 indexed thresholds in his pricing sheet and update in January.",penalties:"Loss of QM presumption and full ATR exposure; TILA remedies.",eff:"Final rule Dec 10, 2020; optional March 1, 2021; mandatory Oct 1, 2022; thresholds adjust each Jan 1.",src:[{t:"12 CFR 1026.43(e)(2) (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1026/subpart-E/section-1026.43#p-1026.43(e)(2)"},{t:"FFIEC APOR tables",u:"https://ffiec.cfpb.gov/tools/rate-spread"}],verify:true},
{k:"section_8_respa_kickbacks",n:"RESPA Section 8 Anti-Kickback and Referral Fee Rules (detail)",cite:"12 U.S.C. 2607; 12 CFR 1024.14-1024.15; CFPB Advisory Opinions (Oct 2020 MSAs FAQ; Feb 2023 digital platforms)",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB / HUD (FHA) / state AGs",topic:["origination","real-estate","advertising"],summary:"Section 8 is the rule that most often trips up brokers who are also real estate licensees. It bans giving or accepting anything of value in exchange for referring settlement service business, bans splitting charges other than for services actually performed, and allows only bona fide payments for goods and services actually furnished. The CFPB's 2020 FAQs explain when marketing services agreements, co-marketing, lead purchases, gifts and promotional events cross the line, and its 2023 advisory opinion says online comparison platforms that steer based on payment violate Section 8. Affiliated business arrangements are permitted with disclosure, no required use and returns limited to ownership share.",applies:"Every settlement service provider on a federally related mortgage loan: brokers, lenders, agents, title, escrow, appraisers, inspectors, home warranty companies, lead generators.",key:["Thing of value is read broadly: money, discounts, trips, meals beyond nominal, desk rent above market, free leads, preferential pricing, future business (1024.14(d)).","Referral includes any oral or written action that influences the selection of a provider (1024.14(f)).","Exemptions: payments to attorneys for services, cooperative brokerage fees between real estate agents, normal promotional and educational activities not conditioned on referrals, bona fide compensation to employees (1024.14(g)).","Affiliated business arrangements: disclosure on the prescribed form at referral, no required use (except a required attorney, credit reporting agency or appraiser representing the lender's interest), and only returns on ownership interest (1024.15).","Marketing services agreements: payment must be for actual marketing services at fair market value, not tied to referrals; CFPB 2020 FAQ warns that MSAs structured to disguise referral payments are unlawful.","Lead generation: paying per lead is permitted if the price is for the lead and not conditioned on closing or referral exclusivity; 'warm transfers' that endorse a lender for payment are referrals.","Co-marketing (Zillow-style) must split costs proportionate to each party's actual advertising.","Gifts, raffles and event sponsorship tied to referral volume are kickbacks; nominal items of general distribution are fine.","Fee splits: a broker may not receive a portion of a title or escrow fee; lenders may not mark up third-party fees (Section 8(b)).","Statute of limitations: 1 year for private suits, 3 years for CFPB/AG; criminal exposure for willful violations.","CA also has B&P 10176(g) (DRE discipline for undisclosed commissions) and Civ. Code 1812.? / 10138 limits on referral fees to unlicensed persons."],broker:"Steven should keep a written policy: no cash or gifts to agents or past clients for referrals, closing gifts of modest value given regardless of referral, event sponsorships at documented fair value, and any MSA reviewed by counsel with a fair-market-value memo. Because he is both a real estate broker and mortgage broker, any transaction where his brokerage lists or represents and his mortgage company originates needs the AfBA disclosure at the first referral and no required use. He should never share in title, escrow or home-warranty fees and should price leads at a flat per-lead or impression rate.",penalties:"Criminal: up to $10,000 and 1 year; civil: 3 times the charge to the consumer plus attorney fees; CFPB penalties (recent consent orders: $1.75 million and $200,000 for RESPA kickbacks in 2023); HUD Mortgagee Review Board for FHA lenders; DRE discipline.",eff:"Section 8 since 1974; AfBA rules 1992; CFPB FAQs Oct 7, 2020; advisory opinion Feb 7, 2023.",src:[{t:"CFPB RESPA Section 8 FAQs",u:"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/mortgage-resources/real-estate-settlement-procedures-act/real-estate-settlement-procedures-act-faqs/"},{t:"12 CFR 1024.14 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-12/chapter-X/part-1024/subpart-B/section-1024.14"}],verify:false},
{k:"state_lo_licensing_reciprocity",n:"NMLS Temporary Authority to Operate and Multi-State MLO Licensing",cite:"12 U.S.C. 5117 (SAFE Act Sec. 1518, added by EGRRCPA Sec. 106); 12 CFR 1008.103; state SAFE acts",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"CFPB (standards) / state regulators via NMLS",topic:["licensing"],summary:"There is no true reciprocity for MLO licenses; each state issues its own. Temporary authority lets a qualified MLO originate in a new state for up to 120 days while the application is pending, if the MLO is employed by a licensed company in that state and either has been state-licensed in another state for the prior 30 days or federally registered for the prior 12 months. The national test with Uniform State Content is accepted by all states, so multi-state expansion mainly requires state-specific education hours, a license application and fees in each state, plus company licensing and bonding. NMLS annual renewal (Nov 1 - Dec 31) must be completed separately for every state license.",applies:"State-licensed MLOs and the companies that sponsor them; a company must itself hold the state license (or exemption) before its MLOs can use temporary authority.",key:["Temporary authority duration: 120 days from application submission, or until the state approves or denies, whichever is first (12 U.S.C. 5117(b)).","Eligibility: registered MLO for 1 continuous year, or state-licensed MLO for 30 days; no license denial, revocation or suspension; no cease and desist; no felony conviction ever for certain crimes; employed by a state-licensed company (5117(a)).","Loans originated under temporary authority are treated as licensed originations (5117(c)).","The national test with UST (adopted by all states) eliminates separate state tests; states may still require state-specific PE hours (CA DFPI 2 hours; FL 2 hours; NV, AZ, IL verify).","Company licensing: CA (DFPI CFL or CRMLA, or DRE), IL (RMLA), NV (NRS 645B mortgage company), FL (Ch. 494 broker or lender), AZ (Title 6 broker/banker) each with bonds and net worth.","Branch licensing varies (CA DRE and DFPI, FL, NV and AZ require branch licenses; remote work rules have loosened since 2020).","Renewal: all licenses Nov 1 - Dec 31 in NMLS; CE must be completed before renewal; late renewal with reinstatement until end of February in many states (verify per state).","Sponsorship: each state license must show the current sponsoring company; MLOs may be sponsored by multiple companies only where the state allows (CA DRE permits multiple broker affiliations with disclosure).","Disclosure questions and employment history in the MU4 must be updated within 30 days.","Surrender of a license requires NMLS filing; inactive licenses lapse."],broker:"Steven already holds CA, IL, NV, FL and AZ licenses; if he adds a state (e.g., TX or CO for relocating military clients) he can use temporary authority once Patriot Pacific is licensed there and the application is filed, but he should not take applications until the company license is active. He should calendar the Nov 1-Dec 31 renewal for all five states, complete CE by early December (NMLS posts CE within 7 days), and keep bonds current for each state. Each new MLO he sponsors needs a per-state license and sponsorship request.",penalties:"Unlicensed activity penalties under each state SAFE act (criminal in CA B&P 10139 up to 6 months and $20,000 per violation for brokers; NV, FL, AZ, IL similar), loss of compensation, license denial.",eff:"Temporary authority effective Nov 24, 2019; national test with UST since 2013.",src:[{t:"NMLS temporary authority page",u:"https://mortgage.nationwidelicensingsystem.org/slr/common/Pages/TemporaryAuthority.aspx"},{t:"12 U.S.C. 5117 (Cornell)",u:"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/12/5117"}],verify:true},
{k:"irs_4506c",n:"IRS Form 4506-C (IVES) Tax Transcript Requests and Income Verification",cite:"26 U.S.C. 6103(c); IRS Form 4506-C (Rev. 10-2022) and IVES Participant Guidelines",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"Internal Revenue Service",topic:["origination","privacy"],summary:"Form 4506-C lets a borrower authorize an IRS Income Verification Express Service participant (usually a vendor hired by the lender) to obtain tax return transcripts, W-2 and 1099 transcripts directly from the IRS. Lenders and the GSEs use transcripts to detect income fraud and to verify self-employment income. The form must be completed exactly to IRS specifications or it is rejected, and the taxpayer consent is limited to the uses the borrower authorizes. Since 2023 the IRS requires the 'clean' 4506-C version and electronic signatures through approved IVES channels, and the consent expires 120 days after signing.",applies:"Borrowers whose income is verified with tax transcripts (self-employed, rental income, commission, and most QM files by lender policy); lenders and brokers that collect the form.",key:["Form must list the taxpayer's name and SSN exactly as on the return, the IVES participant, the tax form number (1040, W-2, 1099), the years requested, and the type of transcript (line 6 and 7 options).","Signature date must be within 120 days of IRS receipt; electronic signatures accepted through IVES-approved e-sign vendors.","Only one 4506-C per taxpayer type; joint filers may sign one form; separate forms for business returns (1120, 1065).","Lenders generally require transcripts for 1-2 years for self-employed borrowers; W-2 transcripts suffice for many wage earners under GSE rules (Fannie allows no-transcript for DU validation).","IRS Section 6103(c) consent limits use of the transcript to the loan purpose; redisclosure to third parties outside the transaction is unlawful.","Taxpayer First Act (2019) requires express consent before tax information is shared with anyone other than the original recipient (26 U.S.C. 6103(c) amendment; 'Taxpayer consent' form in every file).","Transcripts show amended returns and IRS adjustments, which can reveal undisclosed income changes.","Non-filers can supply a Verification of Non-Filing letter; extensions must be documented.","Identity theft indicators on IRS records cause rejections; borrower must resolve with the IRS directly.","Borrowers can also pull their own transcripts via IRS online account, which many lenders accept with security features."],broker:"Steven should have borrowers sign the 4506-C and the Taxpayer First Act consent at application, complete it exactly (names as filed, correct years, correct transcript type) to avoid IRS rejections that delay closings 1-2 weeks, and warn self-employed and rental-income borrowers that the transcript must match the returns provided. He should never forward transcripts to a real estate agent or other third party. For late filers in tax season he should plan around IRS processing delays and ask lenders about their non-transcript options.",penalties:"Unauthorized disclosure of tax information: civil damages under 26 U.S.C. 7431 ($1,000 per act or actual damages) and criminal penalties under 7213 and 7216 (up to 5 years for willful disclosure); loan fraud exposure if transcripts are forged.",eff:"Form 4506-C introduced March 1, 2021 (replacing 4506-T for IVES); revised form mandatory March 1, 2023; Taxpayer First Act consent effective Dec 28, 2019.",src:[{t:"IRS Form 4506-C",u:"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-4506-c"},{t:"IRS IVES program",u:"https://www.irs.gov/individuals/income-verification-express-service"}],verify:false},
{k:"patriot_act_cip",n:"USA PATRIOT Act Section 326 Customer Identification Program and Section 314",cite:"31 U.S.C. 5318(l); 31 CFR 1020.220 (banks); 31 CFR 1029 (RMLO AML program); FinCEN guidance for non-bank lenders",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"FinCEN / Treasury",topic:["data-security","origination"],summary:"Section 326 of the PATRIOT Act requires financial institutions to verify the identity of customers opening accounts, keep records of the verification, and check names against government terrorist lists. The formal CIP rule applies to banks, broker-dealers and similar institutions; non-bank mortgage lenders and brokers are not under 31 CFR 1020.220 directly, but their AML program under 31 CFR 1029 must include risk-based customer identification, and every lender they work with imposes a Patriot Act identification form on each borrower. Section 314(a) lets FinCEN query institutions for suspects, and 314(b) lets institutions share information voluntarily. The practical result for a broker is a documented ID check on every borrower and OFAC screening.",applies:"Banks and other 'financial institutions' for the CIP rule; RMLOs for AML program-based identification; all borrowers in practice via lender Patriot Act forms.",key:["Collect name, date of birth, residential address and identification number (SSN or ITIN; for non-U.S. persons a passport or other government ID number) before or at account opening (1020.220(a)(2)(i)).","Verify identity through documents (unexpired government photo ID) or non-documentary methods (credit bureau data, public databases) within a reasonable time (1020.220(a)(2)(ii)).","Record the ID type, number, issuer, dates and the methods and results of verification; retain 5 years after the account closes (1020.220(a)(3)).","Check the customer against lists of known or suspected terrorists provided by the government (OFAC SDN in practice) (1020.220(a)(4)).","Give customers notice that identity information is being requested to comply with federal law (the Patriot Act notice on the application) (1020.220(a)(5)).","Beneficial ownership (customer due diligence rule, 31 CFR 1010.230) applies to banks for legal-entity customers; brokers see it when an LLC borrower opens accounts or when lenders ask for entity ownership on DSCR loans.","Section 314(a): FinCEN requests must be searched within 2 weeks; Section 314(b): voluntary sharing with registration.","ITIN borrowers are permitted; the ITIN is an acceptable identification number.","Lenders' Patriot Act forms typically require two forms of ID and the closing agent to verify at signing."],broker:"Steven should photograph or copy an unexpired government ID at application, confirm the name, DOB, address and SSN/ITIN match the credit report and 1003, and complete the lender's Patriot Act form, noting the verification method in the file. For entity borrowers on DSCR loans he should collect the operating agreement and ownership list up front since lenders will ask. His AML program should describe these steps so his identification and OFAC checks are documented as program elements.",penalties:"FinCEN civil penalties for AML program deficiencies; lender repurchase for undocumented identity; criminal exposure for knowingly facilitating identity fraud.",eff:"PATRIOT Act Oct 26, 2001; CIP rule effective Oct 1, 2003; CDD rule effective May 11, 2018.",src:[{t:"31 CFR 1020.220 (eCFR)",u:"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-31/subtitle-B/chapter-X/part-1020/subpart-B/section-1020.220"},{t:"FinCEN 314(a) program",u:"https://www.fincen.gov/resources/financial-institutions/314a-program"}],verify:false},
{k:"ofac",n:"OFAC Sanctions Screening (Office of Foreign Assets Control)",cite:"50 U.S.C. 1701 et seq. (IEEPA); 31 CFR Chapter V; Specially Designated Nationals list",lvl:"federal",st:"",agency:"Treasury OFAC",topic:["data-security","origination","closing"],summary:"OFAC administers U.S. economic sanctions; every U.S. person, including a mortgage broker, is prohibited from doing business with individuals and entities on the Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons list and with sanctioned countries. There is no explicit OFAC screening rule for brokers, but strict liability for dealing with a blocked person means every lender, title company and GSE requires screening of borrowers, sellers and parties to the transaction. Matches must be investigated and true hits blocked and reported within 10 business days. Screening is usually bundled into the credit report and the AML program.",applies:"All U.S. persons and businesses; mortgage brokers, lenders, title and escrow companies screen borrowers, co-borrowers, sellers, non-borrowing spouses, entities and their owners, and sometimes gift donors.",key:["Prohibited transactions with SDNs and blocked persons; property of blocked persons must be blocked and reported (31 CFR 501.603).","Strict liability: no intent required for civil penalties.","Screen at application and before closing; many lenders rescreen at funding; title companies screen all parties to the deed.","Potential matches: compare name, DOB, address, nationality and ID numbers; document false-positive clearance.","True hits: do not proceed, block the transaction, call the OFAC hotline (800-540-6322) and file a blocking report within 10 business days; annual report of blocked property by Sept 30.","Comprehensive sanctions on Cuba, Iran, North Korea, Syria, Crimea/DNR/LNR regions and extensive Russia-related programs; sanctioned-country nationals are not automatically blocked but ownership and residency matter.","50 Percent Rule: entities owned 50 percent or more by blocked persons are blocked even if not listed.","Rejected (not blocked) transactions involving sanctioned countries must also be reported within 10 business days.","Record retention 5 years (31 CFR 501.601).","Voluntary self-disclosure mitigates penalties."],broker:"Steven should confirm that his credit report includes an OFAC check for each borrower, document clearance of any possible match (common with Hispanic and Middle Eastern names where an alias flags), and let the lender and title company rescreen at closing. For foreign-national and ITIN borrowers he should note country of citizenship and residence early so sanctions issues surface before an appraisal is paid for. If a real hit ever occurs he must stop, not tip off the party, and call OFAC and the lender.",penalties:"Civil penalties up to the greater of about $377,700 per violation (IEEPA, indexed) or twice the transaction value; criminal penalties up to $1 million and 20 years for willful violations.",eff:"IEEPA 1977; Trading with the Enemy Act 1917; SDN list updated continuously.",src:[{t:"OFAC sanctions list search",u:"https://sanctionssearch.ofac.treas.gov/"},{t:"OFAC FAQs",u:"https://ofac.treasury.gov/faqs"}],verify:false}
]);
